window.EXHIBITION.introduction=["The six multimedia works presented here emerged from “Introduction to the Medical Humanities” (CHLA 215), taught at the American University of Beirut in 2025 and 2026. Offered through AUB’s Critical Humanities for the Liberal Arts (CHLA) program, the course forms part of the pedagogical work of the Program on Medical History, Ethics & Politics (MHEP) at the Faculty of Medicine. Drawing on literature, history, philosophy, anthropology, and the arts, it invites students to examine medicine not only as a scientific and clinical enterprise, but also as an ethical, social, political, and lived practice.", "These questions were far from abstract. More than 40 students across the two cohorts studied medicine, illness, and care while living through war and its aftermath in Lebanon. They were asked to reflect on medical responsibility, human vulnerability, and the experience of illness at a time when violence and institutional fragility were reshaping the very conditions of care.", "Through research, oral history, documentary film, sound, images, and personal reflection, the six selected projects explore wounded bodies, interrupted care, trauma, and memory. They ask how war injures bodies, unsettles medical obligations, and leaves traces that endure long after the immediate violence has subsided.", "The six projects were created by students who have since pursued different academic paths: some are now medical students, others are completing their undergraduate studies, and others are graduate students in the Faculty of Arts and Sciences. All six are members of the Medical Humanities Society, which has collaborated with MHEP in bringing this exhibition to life.", "Together, these works form an emerging archive of a particular historical and pedagogical moment: students learning to think critically about medicine in times of crisis while experiencing those crises themselves. They invite us to consider not only what war does to bodies and to medicine, but also what it means to witness, represent, and remember these experiences while history is still unfolding.", "Watch, listen, read, and look closely. Scroll at your own pace, or enter through any individual work."];
Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="war-medicine"),{"poster": "nizar-cover-v2.png", "coverCaption": "People gathered outside the American University of Beirut Medical Center during the September 2024 pager explosions. The image introduces one of the project's central concerns: how quickly ordinary hospital space can be transformed into a site of mass emergency response.", "coverCredit": "Reuters"});

