{const photos=window.EXHIBITION.photographs;const order=['photograph-12','photograph-03','photograph-02','photograph-01','photograph-04','photograph-08','photograph-05','photograph-06','photograph-07','photograph-13','photograph-09','photograph-11','photograph-10'];window.EXHIBITION.photographs=order.map(id=>photos.find(p=>p.id===id));}

Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION,{"mhs": "Established in August 2026 and approved by the Office of Medical Student Affairs and the Office of Student Affairs, the AUB Medical Humanities Society (AUBMHS) brings medicine into conversation with the humanities, social sciences, and arts. The society stems from the spirit of the course CHLA 215 “Introduction to Medical Humanities,” and operates under the Program on Medical History, Ethics, and Politics. Open to all students, it will support a student-led journal and podcast, and organize workshops, film screenings, reading groups, and other initiatives across four thematic areas.", "mhsTagline": "The first student-led medical humanities society in the Middle East.", "mhsAreas": ["Creative Arts, Literature & Narrative Medicine", "Ethics, History, Philosophy & the Future of Medicine", "Society, Identity & Health", "War, Conflict, Displacement & Medicine."]});

window.EXHIBITION.mhs="Founded in August 2026, and approved by both the Office of Medical Student Affairs and the Office of Student Affairs, the AUB Medical Humanities Society (AUBMHS) grew out of CHLA 215 \"Introduction to Medical Humanities,\" and operates under the Program on Medical History, Ethics & Politics. Open to all students, it bridges medicine, the humanities, social sciences, and arts through a student-led journal, podcast, workshops, screenings, and reading groups across four themes:";

Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="the-body-remembers"),{"id": "the-body-remembers", "creator": "Seungju Moon", "title": "The Body Remembers", "src": "body-remembers.m4a", "duration": "29:37", "language": "English", "poster": "body-remembers-cover.webp", "posterAlt": "Painting of a seated man among dense trees, with an axe lodged in a tree trunk.", "coverCaption": "Al Hattab, 1990. Oil on canvas.", "coverCredit": "Abdel Hamid Baalbaki. Source: Dalloul Art Foundation.", "coverSource": "https://dafbeirut.org/collection/abdel-hamid-baalbaki/al-hattab", "synopsis": "An audio documentary exploring how war in Lebanon leaves lasting marks on the body. Through oral histories and research, Seungju Moon examines embodied trauma and the limits of diagnostic labels in representing lived experience.", "statement": "[Artist statement to be supplied]", "credits": "Primary Sources:\n\nDalaa Zaarour, American University of Beirut, 4 April 2025.\n\nCharlotte El-Khalil, AUBMC, 3 April 2025.\n\nSecondary Sources:\n\nBouri, Christina. “Pagers, Walkie-Talkies, and Psychological Warfare in Lebanon.” ThinkGlobalHealth, 10 October 2024. https://www.thinkglobalhealth.org/article/pagers-walkie-talkies-and-psychological-warfare-lebanon.\n\nRussell, Catherine. “War in Lebanon Inflicting Devastating Physical and Emotional Impacts on Children.” UNICEF. 31 October 2024. https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/war-lebanon-inflicting-devastating-physical-and-emotional-impacts-children.\n\nGrondecka, Jagoda. “Two-thirds of Lebanese exhibit at least one mental health disorder due to Israel's war.” The New Arab. 7 November 2024. https://www.newarab.com/features/israels-war-lebanon-causes-surge-mental-health-trauma.\n\nReferences 1-3 are articles that discuss casualties and psychological damages on the Lebanese population during the 2024 war. As mentioned in the podcast, these reports fail to capture the complex somatic manifestations of war trauma. \n\n\n\nKaram, Elie G. et al. “The aftermath of multiple trauma on a nation: unraveling Lebanon’s unique mental health struggle.” Frontiers in Psychiatry 15 (2024). https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1444245\n\nYamout, Rouham. and Chaaya, Monique. “Individual and collective determinants of mental health during wartime. A survey of displaced populations amidst the July–August 2006 war in Lebanon.” Global Public Health 6, no.4 (2011), 354-370. DOI: 10.1080/17441692.2010.494163.\n\nFarhood, Layla, Hassan Dimassi, and Nichole L Strauss. “Understanding Post-Conflict Mental Health: Assessment of PTSD, Depression, General Health and Life Events in Civilian Population One Year after the 2006 War in South Lebanon.” Journal of Trauma Stress Disorders and Treatment 2, no.2 (2013). doi:10.4172/2324-8947.1000103.\n\nFarhood, Laila, Huda Zurayk, Monique Chaya, Fadia Saadeh, Garbis Meshefedjian, and Thuraya Sidani. \"The impact of war on the physical and mental health of the family: The Lebanese experience.\" Social Science & Medicine 36, no.12 (1993): 1555-1567. https://doi.org/https://doi.org/10.1016/0277-9536(93)90344-4. \n\nShaar, Khuzama Hijal.\"Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder in Adolescents in Lebanon as Wars Gained in Ferocity: A Systematic Review.\" Journal of Public Health Research 2, no.2 (2013). https://doi.org/10.4081/jphr.2013.e17. \n\nReferences 4-8 are journal articles that explore the mental health state of Lebanon after the Civil War (1975-1990), the 2006 Lebanon-Israel War, and the 2020 Beirut explosion. Similar to the articles covering the 2024 war, the studies’ scope fails to capture the intricate relationship between somatic symptoms and mental health in the war context. This shows that the gap is a recurring trend in the academic literature of Lebanon, and not only in the 2024 war. \n\n\n\nYehuda, Rachel, and Bierer, Linda M., “The relevance of epigenetics to PTSD: Implications for the DSM-V†.” Journal of Traumatic Stress 22, no.5 (2009): 427-434.  https://doi.org/10.1002/jts.20448\n\nGanzel, Barda, Casey, B. J., Glover, Glover, Voss, Henning U., & Temple, Elise. “The Aftermath of 9/11: Effect of intensity and recency of trauma on outcome.” Emotion 7, no.2 (2007): 227–238. https://doi.org/10.1037/1528-3542.7.2.227\n\nKhan, Zainab, Nour El Messiri, Emann Iqbal, et al. \"On the Role of Epigenetic Modifications of Hpa Axis in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Resilience.\" Journal of Neurophysiology 133, no. 3 (2025): 742-59. https://doi.org/10.1152/jn.00345.2024. \n\nWilker, Sarah, Vanja Vukojevic, Anna Schneider, et al. \"Epigenetics of Traumatic Stress: The Association of Nr3c1 Methylation and Posttraumatic Stress Disorder Symptom Changes in Response to Narrative Exposure Therapy.\" Translational Psychiatry 13, no. 1 (2023): 14. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-023-02316-6. \n\nReferences 9–12 are peer-reviewed scientific journal articles that examine trauma-induced epigenetic changes. These studies offer a biological framework for understanding the long-term effects of war-related trauma.\n\n\n\nDias, Brian G., and Kerry J. Ressler. \"Parental Olfactory Experience Influences Behavior and Neural Structure in Subsequent Generations.\" Nature Neuroscience 17, no. 1 (2014): 89-96. https://doi.org/10.1038/nn.3594. \n\nMulligan, Connie J., Edward B. Quinn, Dima Hamadmad, Christopher L. Dutton, Lisa Nevell, Alexandra M. Binder, Catherine Panter-Brick, and Rana Dajani. \"Epigenetic Signatures of Intergenerational Exposure to Violence in Three Generations of Syrian Refugees.\" Scientific Reports 15, no. 1 (2025): 5945. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-89818-z. \n\nReferences 13 and 14 build on the findings of references 9–12 by showing that trauma-induced changes can be inherited, highlighting the potential transgenerational impact. Not only do these studies underscore the significance of my topic, but they also lay the groundwork for refuting the ‘immune theory’ of war trauma.\n\n\n\nCao-Lei, Lei, Daniel Saumier, Justine Fortin, and Alain Brunet. \"A Narrative Review of the Epigenetics of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder and Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder Treatment.\" Frontiers in Psychiatry 13 (2022). https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.857087. \n\n\n\nReference 15 provides a bright side of epigenetics, suggesting that trauma-induced epigenetic changes are reversible. \n\nConley, Daltom. “A New Scientific Field Is Recasting Who We Are and How We Got That Way.” The New York Times, 13 March 2025. https://www.nytimes.com/2025/03/13/opinion/genetics-nature-nurture-sociogenomics.html\n\nReference 16 introduces the concept of sociogenomics. Applying sociogenomics to Lebanon, I suggest in my podcast that constant violence in Lebanon creates a loop effect that amplifies trauma-induced epigenetic changes.\n\n\n\nMurphy, F. M. \"Gulf War Syndrome.\" Bmj 318, no. 7179 (1999): 274-5. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.318.7179.274.\n\nReference 17 introduces Gulf  War Syndrome, which is an excellent example that shows how war trauma manifests somatically in a very complex manner that does not conform to the standard diagnostic framework. \n\n\n\nAbi Hana, Racha, Maguy Arnous, Eva Heim, Anaïs Aeschlimann, Mirja Koschorke, Randa S. Hamadeh, Graham Thornicroft, et al. \"Mental Health Stigma at Primary Health Care Centres in Lebanon: Qualitative Study.\" International Journal of Mental Health Systems 16, no. 1 (2022): 23. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13033-022-00533-y. \n\nMaalouf, Fadi T., Bibi Alamiri, Samir Atweh, Anne E. Becker, Majda Cheour, Hala Darwish, Lilian A. Ghandour, et al. \"Mental Health Research in the Arab Region: Challenges and Call for Action.\" The Lancet Psychiatry 6, no. 11 (2019): 961-66. https://doi.org/10.1016/S2215-0366(19)30124-5.\n\nReferences 18 and 19 suggest that social stigma against psychiatry is a key obstacle preventing patients from seeking help from psychiatrists in Lebanon and the Arab region. \n\n\n\nMusic Credit: Sur le Quai. Lucid Fall. YouTube. Uploaded by ejj207, April 29, 2009. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5IEFMp_K6nk", "transcript": "The Body Remembers\n\nEpisode Title: Somatic Manifestations of War Trauma in Lebanon Format: Clean-Verbatim Transcript\n\nHost: [music] What if the body never forgets what the mind tries so hard to bury?\n\nWelcome to my podcast, The Body Remembers. This podcast is about how trauma lives not just in the mind, but in the body. In a place like Lebanon, where war has become generational, that truth becomes undeniable.\n\nIn September 2024, Lebanon found itself once again engulfed in violence. The conflict that began earlier in late 2023 on the southern borders rapidly spread into populated areas, sparked by a pager attack, displacing thousands and claiming multitudes of civilian lives. As of December, the Ministry of Public Health of Lebanon has reported 4,047 deaths and more than 16,000 injuries. Yet, the full toll of the war is still being counted—not in the bodies, but in minds and lives left in psychological ruin.\n\nHost: While the world turns its attention to casualty numbers and geopolitical strategies, far less attention is paid to the silent wounds. The body, we know now with advances in psychology and psychiatry, remembers trauma in ways that are just as persistent and devastating as psychological symptoms.\n\nHowever, most reports and articles that I found on the internet or databases—including those from Global Health, UNICEF, and The New Arab, to list a few—focus primarily on physical casualties or reduce the psychological toll of war to a single diagnosis like PTSD. They often treat somatic symptoms and mental health issues as parallel outcomes of the war, as if they are mutually exclusive rather than deeply interconnected.\n\nSome may dismiss this claim, saying that not enough time has passed since the war for studies to be published. Yes, I agree. This, however, is not just about the 2024 war. Lebanon's modern history is punctuated by war-induced or similar traumas: the Civil War from 1975 to 1990, the 2006 war with Israel, and the Beirut explosion of 2020, which is the biggest non-nuclear explosion of the modern era. Each of these has left not only visible destruction, but also invisible legacies of pain.\n\nHost: I reviewed a great number of studies regarding these tragedies, and the trend was the same. Most discuss the psychological impact and somatic health separately. When the literature does talk about mental health after the war, they reduce the conversation to numbers: 47.8% of Lebanese youth now suffer from anxiety; PTSD prevalence rose by 30% post-2020; and so on, without elaboration on the somatic experience. When they do refer to them, like in a study by Dr. Laila Farhood, they simply list the generic physical symptoms of PTSD that match with the DSM-5's definition of PTSD, like sleep disturbances and heartbeat acceleration.\n\nThis is not at all what I've seen as a person who went through the recent war myself. The reality scars that the war left are far more persistent and complex than simple percentages of PTSD. The interplay between body and mind became ever more multifaceted when it came to the war. The way people suffered from the war was each a unique story of their own, but these stories are sadly reduced to like 0.01 percentage of another PTSD case.\n\nHost: This podcast, titled The Body Remembers, is about that interplay between the mind and the body, between what we can name and what we cannot. It's about how the pain of war manifests somatically in ways we often miss. To do so, I will examine the oral histories of the victims of the war, the story that I argue goes missing in academia.\n\nThere will be two interviews, each of which is around eight minutes. Although the actual interview was longer, for the time being, I deeply regret having cut off some of what was said. My approach to the two interviews was rather different. The first interview was with a close friend of mine who has gone through a lot during the war. With her, I purposely limited my questions as much as I could. It was rather a conversational format where I allowed the interviewee to reflect on herself and speak freely of her experience.\n\nThere were moments when the interviewee got too emotional, and we had to halt the interview for a while. On the contrary, the second interview was conducted with a set of questions as it was with a scholar of our topic.\n\nSo let's now hear more from our interviewees, starting with our speaker, Dalaa Zaarour, a student from American University of Beirut.\n\n[music]\n\nHost: Hello.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: Hello.\n\nHost: Can you briefly introduce yourself?\n\nDalaa Zaarour: My name is Dalaa Zaarour. I'm 20 years old, and I'm a bio major in AUB. I'm Lebanese-Palestinian.\n\nHost: Can you tell us about what happened to you during the war in Lebanon?\n\nDalaa Zaarour: Okay, so the war started in September 2024. September 23, they started bombing the south of Lebanon, and it was so like—all of my family lives there except for me and my parents and my brothers. So it was so stressing, and I was in uni, and I was like panicking a lot. And I was calling my family, extended family, and no one was answering, and no one had like connection. So yeah.\n\nAnd then, like the next day—I think the same or the next day—they hit where I live. Me and my parents, we grabbed anything we could, and then we went to Jabal, to Aley, and we stayed there.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: First, we couldn't find a place to stay, so we went to different destinations. So first we went to different destinations in the district, in Aley district, until we settled in a place called Mtein or something—Mtein. So yeah.\n\nSo this was the first two days of the war. After that, like when we settled and we kept on going back to Beirut to get our stuff up until until we received the news that they bombed Zuqaq al-Blat. And sadly, some of my family members were there, and some of them sadly passed away.\n\nSo after that, like towards the end of the war, we received news that our house got destroyed, our building. And then the ceasefire was announced. Okay, and then me and my family stayed a bit in Aley, and then we went back, and we found a new place to settle in.\n\nHost: I know it's not easy to reflect and share this kind of experiences, but could you share more and elaborate more on how you felt during the war, what kind of things you went through, and how were people around you? Did they go through similar stuff like you?\n\nDalaa Zaarour: So during the war, my family and my extended family, we were all distributed across all parts of Lebanon. And of course, everyone was in danger because there were so many places that were getting bombed. And as you know, uni was hybrid, so I had to go to Beirut for some time so I could attend classes in uni because some professors required us to do so.\n\nBut at the same time, my parents were in a different part of Lebanon; they were in Jabal. So I was so anxious all the time, and I was so stressed and scared that something would happen to them, and they might get hurt, or they might get bombed or something.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: And this reflected on my physical health as well. I had no appetite, and my parents were so worried to the point—I used to live with my aunt in Beirut. So my parents were so worried after she told them, so I was rushed to the ER, and they had to give me IV bags. They had to give me IV bags.\n\nAnd so after that, I went back home, and my physical health was still in a very weak state. I started feeling numbness in the left side of my body, shortness of breath, basically the symptoms that would feel like you are having a heart attack. So I was rushed to the ER again for a second time, and the doctor that was present in the ER told me that my body was faking a heart attack due to severe trauma from the war.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: And then I was referred to a psychiatrist, but I thought about it, and I didn't want to make my parents worry, so I was reluctant to go. So even though I knew that visiting a psychiatrist would help me a lot, I did not go because often people in our society—people who visit the psychiatrists in our society are stigmatized and often stereotyped as weird people.\n\nSo after the ceasefire, I thought my life was going to go back to normal, but it appeared that my body had been severely affected, and my mental state had been severely affected by this war. So anytime anyone would talk loudly, or someone would close the door and it would make a loud noise, or whenever I'm driving around and someone beeps at me, I would get startled, I'd get anxious, my heart rate starts racing as if it's going to jump out of my chest, and it starts squeezing as well.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: So before the war, I used to have really strong immunity, and whenever I used to get sick, it would only last for about like two or three days. But after the ceasefire, I've caught the flu like three times, and I had severe symptoms. They were very strong, so I was hospitalized.\n\nAs for people around me, some of them did go through the same things that I went through, especially my family and my cousins. But this wasn't the case for my foreigner friends or my uni friends that have lived abroad. Although they went through displacement and so on, after the ceasefire, they seemed fine. Like, they seemed okay.\n\nDalaa Zaarour: Maybe because my family and I, as Lebanese-Palestinians, we went through a lot. So my parents have lived through almost every single war that happened in Lebanon, be it the Civil War, the 1982 war, the 2006, and as well as the recent one in 2024. And my grandparents are victims of the Nakba in 1948.\n\nAnd I remember when we were kids, my grandma used to gather us around and tell us stories from that period of time. I remember she told us that they didn't even have time to gather essential belongings, so they just fled on foot from Palestine to the borders.\n\nSo yes, maybe those reoccurring senses of fear caused me and my family to suffer more physically and mentally from the war.\n\nHost: [music] It is truly not easy to listen to those stories of pain, especially of your close ones. But I believe it is through these stories that we advance in understanding of our body and mind, so as to provide correct support for these victims.\n\nNext, we will be hearing from a clinical psychologist in the AUBMC, Ms. Charlotte Al-Khalil, who has been working closely with the displaced people in Lebanon since the onset of the war.\n\n[music]\n\nHost: Can you introduce yourself very quickly?\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: Yeah. So I'm Charlotte Khalil, psychologist. I work here at the American University of Beirut Medical Center, and I was leading the initiative of the department. We were providing mental health support for the displaced in two shelters here around AUB.\n\nHost: So can you please share your personal encounters with patients who have suffered from war psychologically, and how this psychological impact has manifested physically?\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: In my work with patients affected by war and trauma in general, I can say that trauma isn't just a story we tell or even a memory we hold. It lives in the body. That's what we know. So many patients present with physical symptoms that seem to have no clear medical cause, like chronic pain, like sleep problems, fatigue, muscle pain.\n\nAnd during the war, I would like to give an example here. During the war, a client presented with chest tightness. Every time a loud noise occurred, she used to feel this pain. Medically, everything was okay. Nothing wrong with her heart, her body. But what we realized after starting therapy is that her body had learned to associate sound with danger.\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: And we know that the body keeps the score and the body will react. So react through muscular problems with like any kind of physical reaction. That's why when we implemented therapy, she noticed a reducing in the like physical symptoms.\n\nAnd you asked me about my clinical work, so I witnessed firsthand how psychological trauma often, even when we are not aware of the impact—because you can encounter people, especially here in Lebanon, we have a lot of defense mechanisms. So, \"We are okay. We don't—we are not afraid,\" you know? And at the end, we react differently. What we cannot tell and express verbally, our body will tell this way.\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: So I think I can relate both in this way. And I would like also to give an example about an adolescent, because when we talk about psychosomatic, we think that it's just related to adults. That's not true. We witness it within kids, within adolescents.\n\nA 13-year-old boy came to therapy for severe stomach pain. The parents did all the medical exams and they didn't find anything. And after therapy, it was really related to the bombing and what they had to endure as a family during the war.\n\nSo I think it's there. The connection is there. The mind and body are very well connected, and we can tell here that the body becomes a silent carrier of unprocessed fear and loss.\n\nHost: As a psychologist, what is your opinion on how PTSD or similar mental illness in the context of war affect physical health?\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: So trauma literally reshapes both brain and body. That's what we know about it. It disrupts the nervous system, affects the hormone levels, and keeps individuals in a state of hyperarousal or even numbness, so it depends on the reaction. This can lead to long-term physical health issues. We can, I think, see cardiovascular disease, autoimmune conditions, chronic pain like we mentioned before.\n\nAnd what we know that feeling safe is a main need for human beings. So when they don't feel safe and inside their own skin, they will react. That's why I do believe that addressing mental health also requires helping people reconnect with their bodies in a safe way and in a safe environment. And that's what is very challenging when you do intervention in a country like Lebanon or in a war setting.\n\nHost: Thank you. So as a scholar in the field of clinical psychology, do you think this connection between body and mental illness and the complex physical symptoms beyond PTSD is done enough, or are they quite limited?\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: I do believe that we need a lot of work to be done on this subject because studies are like—okay, we know that when we describe symptoms, when we talk about trauma and PTSD, we describe a lot of symptoms related to physical reactions, but it's not enough. I think we should combine both and control for both.\n\nAnd we need a lot of work on this to be able to understand this connection, not just to discover it—we know it's there—but maybe to tailor intervention and, you know, to also guide our intervention as psychologists and even as, you know, physicians.\n\nHost: Yes. Thank you.\n\nCharlotte Al-Khalil: So I think it's very important to address the body's response to trauma because if we want to support people and communities affected by war, we need to integrate body-based and trauma-informed approaches both together. So ignoring the somatic experience of trauma limits our ability to truly help, I think, because we will miss a lot of details there.\n\nAnd healing involves restoring a sense of control, reconnecting with the body, and building secure connections with others. So it's a whole intervention that we should implement to be able to really do an effective intervention. And to do that, we need a lot of studies. And to do this, I think a collaboration is needed between different departments to be able to find the correct response to that.\n\nHost: Okay. Thank you so much.\n\n[music]\n\nHost: What we've just heard reinforces a difficult truth: the body becomes a silent carrier of unprocessed fear, and the somatic manifestation of those triggers become very complex in the context of war.\n\nAnd I must mention that the stories we've heard today in our podcast are only the tip of an iceberg. I volunteered in many events that aided the displaced people during the war, especially in medical aids, and I've seen a lot more—from a girl in her teens suffering from sudden urination, to a young woman losing almost all her hair.\n\nYet in most cases, the unique experiences of patients are simply classified under PTSD using rigid, top-down framework, usually by matching a patient's symptoms to a checklist in the DSM. Perhaps this is another example of drawbacks of medicalization, where we try to fit every illness experience into diagnostic names written in the guideline like in the DSM-5, regardless of the complexity and diversity of illness experienced by the victims of the war.\n\nHost: According to the DSM-5, PTSD is defined as exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, followed by symptoms such as intrusive thoughts, negative mood, sleep dysfunction, or hyperarousal. But what if your symptoms are different? What if your trauma expresses through irritable bowel syndrome, immune dysfunction, or chronic chest pain, like the example given by Mrs. Charlotte Khalil?\n\nLet's consider a case of Gulf War syndrome. After the 1991 Gulf War, thousands of veterans reported a range of symptoms that included memory loss, joint pain, rashes, and chronic fatigue. For years, their complaints were dismissed, but today, the US Department of Veterans Affairs recognizes Gulf War syndrome as a legitimate multi-symptom illness. This shows how war can alter the body in profound and unpredictable ways that do not necessarily match with the existing classifications in the DSM.\n\nHost: Without this understanding, we cannot provide pertinent intervention and, to borrow what Ms. Khalil said, not just to discover, but to tailor intervention. We need more studies, especially in the region of constant violence like in Lebanon, not just in psychology, but also in the field of medicine and biology.\n\nAnd this brings us to a critical field of research: epigenetics and sociogenomics. Epigenetics refers to a study of how environmental factors, including trauma, can change gene expression. According to a 2009 study by Dr. Yehuda and his colleagues, trauma survivors showed epigenetic changes related to stress hormone regulation, particularly in genes that affect the amygdala and HPA axis, which is the biological stress response system. It is not a single study; multiple studies done on different trauma experiences, such as 9/11 terror or Holocaust or personal trauma experiences, show the same results.\n\nHost: Even more shockingly, some of these epigenetic changes appear to be inheritable, according to a study done by Dr. Dias in 2013 and by Dr. Mulligan and his colleagues in their study on Syrian refugees.\n\nThe sociogenomic model takes this one step further. It suggests that trauma-based epigenetic changes can influence how entire communities respond to stress. According to Dalton Conley, the author of The Social Genome: The New Science of Nature and Nurture, these inherited markers can feed back into cultural narratives, shaping behaviors, identities, and even further risk of psychiatric illness. This is a loop effect: trauma reinforcing trauma biologically and socially.\n\nPerhaps this is related to what our first interviewee, Tala, said about how her family lineage saturated with war could be the culprit that impacted her and her family significantly more than others. This is especially alarming because it goes against what is commonly believed—that people become immune to trauma; those who survive the war will be more numb and okay with experiencing another one, as they have already gone through one. However, it seems that it is the exact opposite.\n\nHost: What is promising, however, is that our expanded knowledge in epigenetics and sociogenomics can be a power, as Dalton Conley says in his essay. A study done by Dr. Anne Brunet and his colleagues on animals showed that epigenetic changes are reversible. Also, the understanding of sociogenomics can guide for how we treat patients in the region of Lebanon, helping to create appropriate, safe environments for victims of the war.\n\nUnfortunately, a lot of obstacles must first be addressed to further such research and investigation in Lebanon. The first is the embedded stigma in Lebanese society. Our first interviewee, Tala, explicitly stated that she refused to visit a psychiatrist due to the stigma towards people who visit the psychiatry department. And this barrier is also well-documented in many studies. In 2022, Dr. Abi Doumit released her study that said that stigma is the number one factor that makes patients reluctant to visit psychiatrists, and this is a clear obstacle towards this kind of research. The same is for many other Arab countries due to deeply tied, conservative religious beliefs of the region, according to Dr. Zeinoun.\n\nHost: Also, as a resident here in Lebanon, it seems that the silence of academia is rationalized by the frequency and commonness of the war scar, where people say everyone experiences it, everyone goes through it, it's not a big of a deal. That is why we need an interdisciplinary endeavor, not just in psychology or biology, but also in social dimensions.\n\nIf the body remembers, then we must listen. The somatic effects of war trauma, from chronic pain to epigenetic inheritance, are not marginal symptoms. They're central to lived experience of survivors. We cannot simply take a top-down approach in reducing these sufferings as mere symptoms of another PTSD. Treating them requires more than just medication or talk therapy. It demands a whole-body approach, one that honors the unique expressions and experiences of trauma across individuals and communities.\n\nIn Lebanon, the burden is especially heavy—not just because of the frequency of wars, but because of the silence that surrounds its aftermath. We must invest in trauma-informed care, in culturally competent research, and in an interdisciplinary healing practices that bridge psychiatry, neuroscience, and social policy. We cannot afford to ignore what our body remembers.\n\n[music]\n\nHost: And with that, I would like to end with some personal remarks on this podcast. Preparing this podcast meant a lot to me, as it was a part of my experience. I was also affected by the war, as my family and my house were very near the bombed areas of Lebanon.\n\nAlthough it was edited off, each interview was accompanied by a personal conversation of mine with the interviewees, sharing my experiences and their experiences in a deeper level. This helped me to reflect back on my experience and bring peaceful period to my traumatic experiences.\n\nIn addition, as a pre-med student, I hope to bring this lesson of seeing patients in whatever context as unique stories to my future practice. Although I have argued that very limited studies are done on interplay between the body and mind in the context of war, I was very delighted to meet Ms. Charlotte Khalil, who shared the same critical view on field and was actually working on a research paper on this topic.\n\nLastly, I thank Dr. Joelle Abi-Rached for providing this great opportunity to prepare this podcast. Just like my title, The Body Remembers, my body will remember this course and this podcast.\n\nThank you for listening.\n\n[music]\n\n", "contentNote": "Includes personal testimony about war, displacement, bereavement, and physical and psychological distress.", "approved": true});

window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="the-body-remembers").statement="The selection of this topic came from a personal reason: wanting to reflect on what affected me most during my senior year of university, the 2024 war in Lebanon. Through personal reflection and listening to the oral histories of others, I wanted to make sense of the effects the war had imposed on me. During my volunteer work, among my friends, and in myself, I witnessed how psychological suffering could also be experienced physically. These observations led me to explore the relationship between body and mind in the aftermath of war.\n\nI chose oral history to approach this question through lived experience. I interviewed Dalaa Zaarour, a close friend and undergraduate student at the American University of Beirut, and Charlotte El-Khalil, a clinical psychologist at the American University of Beirut Medical Center. With Dalaa, I kept my questions open, allowing her to guide the conversation and pausing when recounting her experiences became difficult. With Charlotte, I used more structured questions to explore the connections between psychological trauma, physical symptoms, and clinical care.\n\nBringing these testimonies into conversation with research, I wanted to examine how embodied experiences of war can resist reduction to a single diagnostic label, such as post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). The project also considers how stigma and the normalization of recurring violence can make suffering harder to acknowledge and support harder to seek. My aim was to keep individual experiences at the centre of these questions.\n\nUltimately, The Body Remembers calls for an understanding of war trauma that considers body and mind together. By recording and listening to oral histories, I hope to preserve experiences that might otherwise remain unheard and encourage further inquiry into what war leaves behind in people’s lives.";

window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="the-body-remembers").synopsis="The Body Remembers explores how war in Lebanon leaves lasting traces in the body. Through conversations with Dalaa Zaarour and clinical psychologist Charlotte El-Khalil, Seungju Moon brings personal testimony into dialogue with research to examine embodied trauma, the limits of diagnostic labels, and the barriers to seeking care.";

// Rebuilt Lives: supplied final materials, 10 October 2026.
Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="rebuilt-lives"),{"src": "rebuilt-lives.m4a", "poster": "rebuilt-lives.png", "posterAlt": "A landscape illustration depicting a prosthetic leg standing on cracked ground, surrounded by emerging green plants and war-damaged buildings. A blue-gloved medical hand gently holds the prosthetic socket, from which a green plant grows. Two white doves fly beneath the Lebanese flag. The words “REBUILT LIVES” appear prominently across the sky, alongside the Arabic word “الأمل” (hope).", "coverCaption": "Rebuilt Lives — A symbol of hope, resilience, and recovery in Lebanon, illustrating how prosthetics can help survivors rebuild their lives amid the destruction of war.", "coverCredit": "Hawraa Ayoub (AI-generated illustration)", "duration": "27:09", "language": "English", "approved": true, "synopsis": "From the 2006 war and the 2020 Beirut port explosion to the 2024 pager attacks, Lebanon has experienced repeated mass-casualty events that have placed enormous pressure on its healthcare system and left survivors with life-altering injuries. While emergency medical responses prioritize saving lives, the long-term challenges faced by survivors, particularly amputees, raise critical questions about who is responsible for ensuring access to prosthetic care, rehabilitation, and continued recovery.\nRebuilt Lives explores the relationship between immediate medical intervention and long-term recovery through secondary research and two interviews conducted with specialists in Lebanon. Dr. George Abi Saad, Head of Trauma Services, Critical Care Surgery, and the Surgical ICU at the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC), discusses hospital preparedness, triage systems, and the lessons learned from previous mass-casualty events, including the challenges of managing overwhelming demand for medical imaging during the pager attacks.\n\nThe podcast then turns to the experiences of amputees through a conversation with prosthetics specialist Bassam Singer, who discusses the physical, emotional, and financial dimensions of rehabilitation. Although advanced technologies, including myoelectric and microprocessor-controlled prosthetics, are available in Lebanon, access remains limited by high costs, dependence on imported components, and insufficient governmental support.\n\nBy linking the urgency of saving lives to the long process of rebuilding them, Rebuilt Lives examines the inequalities embedded in Lebanon's healthcare system and questions the gap between medical innovation and access. Ultimately, the podcast asks whether access to prosthetic care and rehabilitation in Lebanon is recognized as a fundamental right or remains a privilege determined by one's ability to pay.", "statement": "My interest in prosthetics began six years ago, but for a long time, I believed that Lebanon’s economic and structural challenges made access to advanced prosthetic technologies almost impossible. Discovering that these technologies were available in Lebanon raised a more important question: are they equally accessible to everyone?\n\nRebuilt Lives emerged from this question and my interest in understanding the relationship between war, healthcare, and rehabilitation. I wanted to explore two interconnected stages that I believe should never be considered separately: the preparedness and response of hospitals during mass casualty events, and the long-term rehabilitation of survivors.\n\nI chose to approach this subject through interviews with two specialists working at different stages of this process. Their experiences allowed me to understand both the technical and human dimensions of trauma care, while challenging some of my earlier assumptions about Lebanon’s healthcare system.\n\nThrough the podcast, I combined their perspectives with findings from my literature review and my own reflections. What stood out to me was the contrast between the expertise and technologies available in Lebanon and the structural and financial barriers limiting access to them.\n\nI hope visitors leave questioning what recovery truly means beyond immediate survival, and whether access to rehabilitation and prosthetics should remain a privilege rather than a fundamental right.", "transcript": "Rebuilt Lives:\n\nPodcast Transcript | English Voice-over Edition\n\nHost, narrator, and English voice-over: Hawraa Ayoub\n\nInterviewees: Dr. George Abi Saad and prosthetics specialist Bassam Singer\n\nOriginal interview language: Arabic\n\nTranscript language: English\n\nEditorial note: Both interviews were conducted in Arabic. The English passages attributed to the specialists below are Hawraa Ayoub's voice-over renderings of their responses, not verbatim English statements by the interviewees. Timestamps are approximate and have not been verified against the audio. The transcript follows the supplied written script, with light punctuation and grammatical editing.\n\nPart I — Introduction\n\n[00:00–05:17] Opening narration\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nThere are the stories we tell during war — and the stories we forget to tell after.\n\nThe headlines often speak in numbers: airstrikes, casualties, destruction.But they rarely speak of silence — of the long, invisible struggle that begins when the bombing ends.\n\nThat silence is what drew me to this project.My name is Hawraa Ayoub. I’m a chemistry pre-med student at the American University of Beirut, and I’ve always been deeply interested in the intersection between prosthetics and medicine — how science can restore not just limbs, but lives.This project attempt to explore that intersection, and to tell the stories of survival, healing, and hope.\n\nThis project is about that silence. About the journey that starts after survival—about the hospitals that must respond in chaos, and the survivors who must rebuild their lives with prosthetics when the war fades from the news.\n\nIn Lebanon, this story is heartbreakingly familiar.From the 2006 war, to the 2020 Beirut port explosion, and most recently the 2024 pager attack that injured almost 3,000 people, conflict continues to carve deep marks into the bodies and lives of civilians. In the chaos of mass casualty events, emergency rooms fill, surgeries begin, and somewhere along the way, decisions are made—some lives are saved, and some limbs are not.\n\nAccording to Reem Karami and Jamal Hoballah in their chapter Amputations and Prostheses, 70% of war injuries affect the musculoskeletal system, particularly the limbs. For many of these patients, amputation is not a last resort—it’s a lifesaving decision. In battlefield conditions, it's often the quickest path to stability, especially when faced with complex blast injuries. But the story doesn’t end with amputation. In many ways, that’s where it begins.\n\nMy project focuses on two critical stages:first, the preparedness of hospitals during trauma injuries, and second, the long road of post-war rehabilitation using prosthetics.\n\nHospital readiness is often the line between life and death.When hospitals like LAUMC-RH received dozens of victims after the 2024 pager explosion, 33 out of 38 patients needed immediate surgery, according to Joelle Kalaji and her colleagues.But emergency care is only one half of the story.The other half is what happens after: when patients leave the operating room missing a part of themselves, and must face a long and costly recovery.\n\nOver the years, as Nour Aoun and Maryam Tajvar (2024) show, Lebanon’s healthcare system has become more and more privatized, meaning that private hospitals and private payments now dominate. Today, over 85% of healthcare costs must be paid directly by patients, making treatment and prosthetics far too expensive for most people.\n\nYet, history tells us something powerful. As Robert Gailey (2007) explains, wars have driven innovation in prosthetics — from wooden limbs to today’s brain-controlled neuroprosthetics. But while the technology has advanced, its production remains unequal. In places like Lebanon, prosthetic devices are available, but they’re almost entirely imported. Local manufacturing is extremely limited, making access costly and dependent on external supply chains.\n\nThis project studies that gap — the space between emergency response and long-term healing.It asks: how do we prepare hospitals for mass trauma? And more importantly, do we treat prosthetics as a basic human right... or as a luxury only a few can afford?\n\nIt’s about systems that break — and people who don’t.It’s about trauma, and it’s about technology.But above all, it’s about the simple yet profound belief that everyone deserves a second chance.Not just to survive — but to walk, to move, and to truly live again.\n\nPart II — Hospital Preparedness and Mass Casualty Response\n\n[Approximately 05:17] Interview introduction\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nSo first To explore how hospitals in Lebanon respond to trauma and mass casualty events, I began by speaking with someone who sees it all from the front lines.\n\nDr. George Abi Saad is the Head of Trauma Services, Critical Care Surgery, and the Surgical ICU at the American University of Beirut Medical Center.I spoke with him, about how hospitals in Lebanon prepare  and respond to mass casualty events.\n\n\n\nWhen disaster strikes, trauma teams face enormous pressure. Every second counts.So I asked Dr. Abi Saad: how do hospitals like AUBMC prepare for such emergencies? What happens in those first critical moments after the patients arrive?\n\nHere’s our conversation.\n\n[Approximately 05:57] Interview response — English voice-over\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\nAt the American University of Beirut Medical Center, we place a strong emphasis on trauma preparedness. One of the key tools we rely on is the Advanced Trauma Life Support course — or ATLS. It’s a two-day intensive training program originally developed in the U.S. which I adopted and implemented here at AUBMC. We teach the course to our residents who are specializing with us, as well as to doctors working in the emergency department — to ensure they’re fully prepared to handle trauma cases using a unified and systematic approach. Nurses also take a parallel course, Advanced Trauma Care for Nurses (ATCN),\n\nThese course prepares doctors and nurses to handle any trauma situation — whether the patient has been in a car crash, injured in a bombing, or is suffering from burns, stabbings, or blunt force injuries. The goal is for every doctor and nurse to follow a clear, systematic approach from the moment the patient arrives.\n\nWhen a mass casualty event occurs, we activate our triage system. The ER is divided into four zones:\n\nRed Zone: for patients who need immediate surgery to survive — for example, someone bleeding from the neck.\n\nYellow Zone: for serious injuries that can wait briefly, like abdominal wounds without active bleeding.\n\nGreen Zone: for minor injuries such as glass cuts or superficial shrapnel wounds.\n\nBlack Zone: for those who are already deceased or beyond saving.\n\nEach zone is staffed by a full medical team: an attending physician, a nurse, a resident, and medical students. Triage officers are responsible for deciding which patient goes where, based on the severity of their condition. And when surgery is required, the trauma team calls in the appropriate specialist: orthopedic surgeons for limb injuries, cardiothoracic surgeons for chest trauma, neurosurgeons for brain injuries, and so on.\n\nThey don’t call every doctor. They directly contact the heads of 7–8 critical divisions, who then mobilize their teams.\n\nThis structure ensures that teams work in parallel, not chaos. Without it, you cannot manage hundreds of patients — like the 370 people injured in the Beirut blast. Each team focuses on its zone, and senior leaders, like the heads of the emergency and surgery departments, circulate constantly to monitor everything. Behind all of this is a clearly defined Emergency Preparedness Plan (EPP)\n\n\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\n\n\nBut no plan is ever perfect. I wanted to know — does this strategy change from one crisis to another? After a mass casualty event like the Pager explosion, did the team  find weaknesses in the system? And if so, how do they fix them?\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\nDuring the Pager explosion, one major challenge we faced was imaging. At the time, our ER had only one CT scanner, and we received over 180 patients with shrapnel injuries to the eyes and limbs — all requiring immediate scans. The other two CT scanners in the hospital, located in the main imaging department downstairs, were still operating on a normal schedule for outpatient appointments, so we couldn’t rely on them.\n\nAfter that experience, we revised our emergency protocol. Now, in the event of another mass casualty incident, we plan to immediately cancel all outpatient appointments and clear all three CT scanners — the one in the ER and the two downstairs — so they’re fully dedicated to trauma patients. With this setup, we can triple our imaging capacity in a crisis and avoid the bottlenecks we experienced that day.\n\nA decade ago, we didn’t have ultrasound in the ER, so identifying internal bleeding was difficult. Today, we use the FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) scan — a rapid bedside ultrasound that allows us to quickly detect organ damage and bleeding, helping us make faster, life-saving decisions.Blood readiness was another challenge. In the past, we’d have to wait to test blood types before transfusions — but bleeding patients can’t wait. So we launched a Massive Transfusion Protocol that ensures blood and platelets, including O-negative, are prepared in advance. It’s one of the most important upgrades we’ve made.Each time we find a weakness, we build a solution. For example, we created a task force called FEMEA to study delays between ER admission and surgery. Their goal is to reduce that time from 45 minutes to 15 minutes. It’s a huge effort — but it can mean the difference between saving a life or not.\n\nWe also run regular simulation drills, where we act out a full-scale mass casualty scenario to test our systems. We identify delays — maybe the elevator gets stuck, maybe imaging backs up — and we fix them before the next real emergency.\n\nWe don’t just hope to be ready. We train for it, over and over again.”\n\n[Approximately 10:57] Building a stronger healthcare system\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nHearing how each experience led to concrete improvements thst made me wonder about the bigger picture.Beyond individual hospitals, can Lebanon — after everything it has endured — build a healthcare system that truly supports trauma survivors?” \n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\n“I believe Lebanon can build an effective healthcare system — but it requires organization and national coordination. It’s not just about how many hospitals or doctors we have; what important  is how we structure and manage the system.\n\nThe Ministry of Health cannot do everything alone. Universities, research centers, and medical institutions need to collaborate. We must conduct field assessments to study hospital readiness — looking at operating rooms, emergency protocols, and equipment — and identify gaps.\n\nThere’s no such thing as 'it can’t be fixed.' Every crisis we’ve faced — from the 2006 war to the Beirut blast — has taught us lessons. During the  2006 war, we performed surgeries underground for 33 days. During the Beirut explosion, hospitals and medical staff were themselves victims — yet we continued to operate.\n\nEach mass casualty event must be treated as an opportunity to improve, not just to recover.\n\nTrauma doesn’t only affect the wounded — it affects families, communities, and society as a whole. One injury can ripple through an entire family’s future.\n\nSo yes, Lebanon can have a strong trauma care system. But it requires smarter planning, stronger emergency preparedness plans like the EPP, faster protocols, and continuous training.\n\nWe don’t wait for the next disaster to learn.We simulate.We adapt.Because every second matters — and every life is worth saving.”\n\n[Approximately 12:57] Transition to prosthetic rehabilitation\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\n\n\nWhat stood out to me in Dr. Abi Saad’s words was not just resilience — but a deep belief in learning from every crisis.Building a stronger healthcare system isn’t just about surviving disasters. It’s about being ready, staying ready, and protecting every life — no matter the circumstances.\n\nBut survival is only the beginning. For many patients, the real journey starts after the emergency — when they must learn to live with an amputation and navigate the long road of physical and emotional recovery.\n\nTo better understand what happens after the trauma, I spoke with Specialist Bassam Singer, the owner of Center Singer for Prosthetics and Orthotics.We talked about the challenges of accessing prosthetic care in Lebanon, the technologies that are available, and the deep personal impact of losing — and regaining — mobility.\n\n\n\nPart III — Prosthetics, Rehabilitation, and Access to Care\n\n[Approximately 14:27] Prosthetics and orthotics\n\nDr. Singer began by explaining the foundation of his work — and the difference between prosthetics and orthotics.\n\nAt our center, we specialize in two main areas: prosthetics, which deal with amputations, and orthotics, which support patients with conditions like scoliosis, paralysis, or neuromuscular disorders. In those cases, we create customized orthopedic devices to help with posture, mobility, and muscle compensation.\n\nBut prosthetics and orthotics alone are not enough. Our work always includes physiotherapy because every device must be paired with physical preparation and retraining. Before fitting a prosthetic, we begin strengthening the body, improving balance, and teaching the patient how to adapt. This applies to every patient, but especially to children with conditions like cerebral palsy. \n\nFrom a technology standpoint, we’re fully up to date. We use CAD-CAM systems and 3D scanning to create customized devices based on the patient's exact measurements. For upper limbs, we use myoelectric prosthetics. We place electrodes on the muscles, and when the brain sends a signal, like “open hand” — the prosthetic responds. in some casesb If the nerves in the arm stump are too weak, we reroute signals from a stronger area — like the shoulder — to restore control.\n\nFor lower limbs, we use microprocessor-controlled prosthetics. These are programmed through dedicated software and can even be adjusted using a mobile app. \n\nThese technologies give us powerful tools to rebuild motion.\n\nWe also offer aesthetic prosthetics in special cases. After the Pager explosion, for example, we treated a patient who had lost several fingers. In those situations, we create silicone replicas that match the original hand in shape, color, texture, and even hair. These prosthetics aren’t functional — they don’t move — but they restore confidence and visual normalcy.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nDr. Singer’s team is clearly at the cutting edge — but not every patient has access to that level of care. So I asked him about the cost of prosthetics in Lebanon, and who can actually afford them.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\nThe price of a prosthetic depends on many factors — the patient’s age, physical condition, lifestyle, and whether they need something basic or advanced. Devices can range from $1,500 Up to one hundred ten thousand U.S. dollars $110,000.\n\nIf a 70-year-old patient needs a leg just to get to the bathroom, we recommend something simple. But if a young, active person comes in — and they can afford it — we fit them with a smart prosthetic. If not, we offer a basic, mechanical one that still meets essential needs.\n\nFor children, it’s more complicated. They outgrow their prosthetics quickly. The socket needs to be replaced every three months. The entire limb — every six months. That makes pediatric care much more expensive, even if we don’t use advanced technology.\n\nAnd unfortunately, there’s no governmental support. The Lebanese Army is the only exception, and even their budget is extremely limited now. Social Security and the Ministry of Health don’t classify prosthetics as essential. They treat it as a secondary or non-essential service—what they often call an “extra” or add-on—so they don’t allocate budget for it.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nDespite all the technology and skill, Dr. Singer emphasized that the most powerful part of his work isn’t mechanical — it’s emotional.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\nA prosthetic isn’t just a medical device. It’s a process — and it’s deeply emotional. Many patients arrive at our center in wheelchairs, emotionally devastated. But when they finally stand up, walk, or regain a small motion — the moment is overwhelming. I’ve seen people cry, laugh, freeze in disbelief. It’s the moment they realize their life is not over.\n\nBut getting to that point isn’t easy. Rehabilitation is the real challenge. The first stage is pre-fitting physiotherapy. We help the patient rebuild strength, balance, and coordination — preparing them to support themselves. Only after this phase do we start training with the prosthetic.\n\nThe entire adaptation process typically takes one week to a month, depending on the person. Most patients visit us three to four times per week, with each session lasting up to four hours. Every case is different, and progress depends on physical strength, psychological readiness, and personal motivation.\n\nFor children, the process is even more demanding. Their bodies are still growing, which means more frequent changes — and more physical and emotional support. But the impact of helping a child walk again? It’s something you never forget.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nAs our conversation came to a close, I asked Dr. Singer to look ahead — not just at the next patient walking through his door, but at the bigger picture. What does the future hold for prosthetic care in Lebanon, a country full of talent but short on support?\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\n\\\n\nThe technology is here. We stay up to date with every advancement. At our center, we follow the global innovation in prosthetics — we use scanning, software, smart systems. We’re ready to lead.\n\nAnd we have the talent. Lebanon has students, engineers, and specialists with incredible potential. I’ve worked with young professionals who could easily compete internationally — not just in fitting prosthetics, but in designing and building them. From a human resources perspective, we’re not behind.\n\nBut even with all our technology and talent, we’re still working within the limitations of a country facing deep economic and structural challenges. Lebanon is a resilient nation — but it’s also a fragile one. And that’s why, today, there’s still no national policy or sustainable funding to support prosthetic care.\n\nIn many countries, the healthcare system ensures that prosthetics are fully covered. Here, patients often have to navigate the process on their own. The public sector does not yet treat prosthetics as a guaranteed right — and that leaves many people behind.\n\nAt the same time, we’re doing what we can with what we have. While we currently import all the electronic components, joints, and materials, we do manufacture the socket locally — the part that connects the prosthetic to the patient’s body. It’s a start. And it’s a sign that with the right investment and coordination, Lebanon could one day expand its local production — reducing costs, increasing access, and becoming a regional leader in prosthetic innovation.\n\nPart IV — Closing Reflections\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nListening to Dr. Singer, one thing becomes clear: even in a fragile system, progress is possible. Piece by piece, socket by socket, a future is being built — not just in technology, but in determination.\n\nProsthetics in Lebanon may still depend on imported parts, but the spirit behind them is entirely local — driven by doctors, engineers, physiotherapists, and patients who refuse to give up.\n\nAs I wrap up this podcast, I find myself thinking less about endings, and more about continuations.\n\nBecause stories like these don’t stop when the patient is discharged. They don’t stop at the prosthetic fitting, or the physical therapy session. They continue — in every step taken, every skill relearned, and every system quietly being improved by those who believe that mobility is a right, not a privilege.\n\nWhat I’ve learned in this journey so far is that Lebanon is not lacking in brilliance. It’s not lacking in drive, in expertise, or in compassion. What it needs is coordination. Vision. And the courage to treat recovery as a collective responsibility.\n\nAnd I’m not done asking questions.\n\nIn the coming weeks, I’ll be continuing this project — not through a podcast series, but through deeper conversations and expanded research. I’ve already scheduled an interview with Dr. Jamal Hoballah, Chairperson and Professor of Surgery at AUBMC. With his expertise in vascular surgery, stents, and reperfusion injury, I want to better understand the decision-making behind amputations — and how the body, and the system, can prepare for healing before the wound even closes.\n\nI’m also planning to reach out to the Center for the Protection of the Rights of the Disabled — to explore the legal, social, and emotional landscapes that shape the lives of amputees after they leave the hospital. What protections exist under Lebanese law? What barriers — financial, infrastructural, or cultural — still stand in the way of full reintegration? I want to understand how issues like health insurance coverage, employment rights, access to public space, and the cost of long-term prosthetic maintenance affect people’s ability not just to survive, but to truly participate in society. Because recovery doesn’t end with physical healing. It continues in the systems that either support or neglect those trying to rebuild their lives.\n\nThis project began with a question about prosthetics. But what it’s really about — is people. Systems. And the silent strength it takes to rebuild not just a limb, but a life.\n\nThank you for listening. And thank you for walking this path with me.\n\nSupplementary Passage — Present in the Supplied Script\n\nThe following passage appears after the closing thanks in the supplied text. Its position in the actual recording is unverified, so it is retained separately rather than assigned an invented timestamp.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nSo even though Lebanon could become a hub for innovation in prosthetics, we’re blocked by economic collapse and lack of government investment. Until that changes, prosthetic care will remain a privilege — not a standard of care.\n\nAs our conversation drew to a close, one thing became clear: Lebanon doesn’t lack knowledge, or skill, or ambition.It lacks structure.It lacks support.And in the world of prosthetics, that gap doesn’t just mean a delayed treatment — it means someone’s future put on hold.\n\nThe technology exists. The talent is here. But without public investment and national policy, prosthetic care will remain a silent crisis — one lived by those who are already carrying more than enough loss.\n\nDr. Singer’s words reminded me that innovation without access is just decoration. And in Lebanon today, mobility — like so much else — too often depends on privilege, not need.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n", "credits": "Hawraa Ayoub\n\nPodcast creator and researcher\n\nHost and narrator\n\nInterviewer\n\nScriptwriter and English voice-over narrator\n\nCover image creator (AI-assisted)\n\nDr. George Abi Saad\n\nInterviewee\n\nTrauma surgeon at the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC)\n\nContributed expertise on hospital preparedness, emergency response, and mass casualty management\n\nDr. Bassam Singer\n\nInterviewee\n\nProsthetics and orthotics specialist, Center Singer\n\nContributed expertise on prosthetic technologies, accessibility, costs, and rehabilitation in Lebanon\n\nInterview with Dr. George Abi Saad, Trauma Surgeon at AUBMC. Interview conducted in Arabic by Hawraa Ayoub; English voice-over by Hawraa Ayoub.\n\nInterview with Bassam Singer, Prosthetics and Orthotics Specialist at Center Singer. Interview conducted in Arabic by Hawraa Ayoub; English voice-over by Hawraa Ayoub."});

Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="rebuilt-lives"),{"src": "rebuilt-lives.m4a", "poster": "rebuilt-lives.png", "approved": true, "duration": "27:09", "language": "English voice-over", "synopsis": "From the 2006 war and the 2020 Beirut port explosion to the 2024 pager attacks, Lebanon has experienced repeated mass-casualty events that have placed enormous pressure on its healthcare system and left survivors with life-altering injuries. While emergency medical responses prioritize saving lives, the long-term challenges faced by survivors, particularly amputees, raise critical questions about who is responsible for ensuring access to prosthetic care, rehabilitation, and continued recovery.\nRebuilt Lives explores the relationship between immediate medical intervention and long-term recovery through secondary research and two interviews conducted with specialists in Lebanon. Dr. George Abi Saad, Head of Trauma Services, Critical Care Surgery, and the Surgical ICU at the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC), discusses hospital preparedness, triage systems, and the lessons learned from previous mass-casualty events, including the challenges of managing overwhelming demand for medical imaging during the pager attacks.\n\nThe podcast then turns to the experiences of amputees through a conversation with prosthetics specialist Bassam Singer, who discusses the physical, emotional, and financial dimensions of rehabilitation. Although advanced technologies, including myoelectric and microprocessor-controlled prosthetics, are available in Lebanon, access remains limited by high costs, dependence on imported components, and insufficient governmental support.\n\nBy linking the urgency of saving lives to the long process of rebuilding them, Rebuilt Lives examines the inequalities embedded in Lebanon's healthcare system and questions the gap between medical innovation and access. Ultimately, the podcast asks whether access to prosthetic care and rehabilitation in Lebanon is recognized as a fundamental right or remains a privilege determined by one's ability to pay.", "statement": "My interest in prosthetics began six years ago, but for a long time, I believed that Lebanon’s economic and structural challenges made access to advanced prosthetic technologies almost impossible. Discovering that these technologies were available in Lebanon raised a more important question: are they equally accessible to everyone?\n\nRebuilt Lives emerged from this question and my interest in understanding the relationship between war, healthcare, and rehabilitation. I wanted to explore two interconnected stages that I believe should never be considered separately: the preparedness and response of hospitals during mass casualty events, and the long-term rehabilitation of survivors.\n\nI chose to approach this subject through interviews with two specialists working at different stages of this process. Their experiences allowed me to understand both the technical and human dimensions of trauma care, while challenging some of my earlier assumptions about Lebanon’s healthcare system.\n\nThrough the podcast, I combined their perspectives with findings from my literature review and my own reflections. What stood out to me was the contrast between the expertise and technologies available in Lebanon and the structural and financial barriers limiting access to them.\n\nI hope visitors leave questioning what recovery truly means beyond immediate survival, and whether access to rehabilitation and prosthetics should remain a privilege rather than a fundamental right.", "coverCaption": "Rebuilt Lives — A symbol of hope, resilience, and recovery in Lebanon, illustrating how prosthetics can help survivors rebuild their lives amid the destruction of war.", "coverCredit": "Hawraa Ayoub (AI-generated illustration)", "posterAlt": "A landscape illustration depicting a prosthetic leg standing on cracked ground, surrounded by emerging green plants and war-damaged buildings. A blue-gloved medical hand gently holds the prosthetic socket, from which a green plant grows. Two white doves fly beneath the Lebanese flag. The words “REBUILT LIVES” appear prominently across the sky, alongside the Arabic word “الأمل” (hope).", "transcript": "Rebuilt Lives:\n\nPodcast Transcript | English Voice-over Edition\n\nHost, narrator, and English voice-over: Hawraa Ayoub\n\nInterviewees: Dr. George Abi Saad and prosthetics specialist Bassam Singer\n\nOriginal interview language: Arabic\n\nTranscript language: English\n\nEditorial note: Both interviews were conducted in Arabic. The English passages attributed to the specialists below are Hawraa Ayoub's voice-over renderings of their responses, not verbatim English statements by the interviewees. Timestamps are approximate and have not been verified against the audio. The transcript follows the supplied written script, with light punctuation and grammatical editing.\n\nPart I — Introduction\n\n[00:00–05:17] Opening narration\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nThere are the stories we tell during war — and the stories we forget to tell after.\n\nThe headlines often speak in numbers: airstrikes, casualties, destruction.But they rarely speak of silence — of the long, invisible struggle that begins when the bombing ends.\n\nThat silence is what drew me to this project.My name is Hawraa Ayoub. I’m a chemistry pre-med student at the American University of Beirut, and I’ve always been deeply interested in the intersection between prosthetics and medicine — how science can restore not just limbs, but lives.This project attempt to explore that intersection, and to tell the stories of survival, healing, and hope.\n\nThis project is about that silence. About the journey that starts after survival—about the hospitals that must respond in chaos, and the survivors who must rebuild their lives with prosthetics when the war fades from the news.\n\nIn Lebanon, this story is heartbreakingly familiar.From the 2006 war, to the 2020 Beirut port explosion, and most recently the 2024 pager attack that injured almost 3,000 people, conflict continues to carve deep marks into the bodies and lives of civilians. In the chaos of mass casualty events, emergency rooms fill, surgeries begin, and somewhere along the way, decisions are made—some lives are saved, and some limbs are not.\n\nAccording to Reem Karami and Jamal Hoballah in their chapter Amputations and Prostheses, 70% of war injuries affect the musculoskeletal system, particularly the limbs. For many of these patients, amputation is not a last resort—it’s a lifesaving decision. In battlefield conditions, it's often the quickest path to stability, especially when faced with complex blast injuries. But the story doesn’t end with amputation. In many ways, that’s where it begins.\n\nMy project focuses on two critical stages:first, the preparedness of hospitals during trauma injuries, and second, the long road of post-war rehabilitation using prosthetics.\n\nHospital readiness is often the line between life and death.When hospitals like LAUMC-RH received dozens of victims after the 2024 pager explosion, 33 out of 38 patients needed immediate surgery, according to Joelle Kalaji and her colleagues.But emergency care is only one half of the story.The other half is what happens after: when patients leave the operating room missing a part of themselves, and must face a long and costly recovery.\n\nOver the years, as Nour Aoun and Maryam Tajvar (2024) show, Lebanon’s healthcare system has become more and more privatized, meaning that private hospitals and private payments now dominate. Today, over 85% of healthcare costs must be paid directly by patients, making treatment and prosthetics far too expensive for most people.\n\nYet, history tells us something powerful. As Robert Gailey (2007) explains, wars have driven innovation in prosthetics — from wooden limbs to today’s brain-controlled neuroprosthetics. But while the technology has advanced, its production remains unequal. In places like Lebanon, prosthetic devices are available, but they’re almost entirely imported. Local manufacturing is extremely limited, making access costly and dependent on external supply chains.\n\nThis project studies that gap — the space between emergency response and long-term healing.It asks: how do we prepare hospitals for mass trauma? And more importantly, do we treat prosthetics as a basic human right... or as a luxury only a few can afford?\n\nIt’s about systems that break — and people who don’t.It’s about trauma, and it’s about technology.But above all, it’s about the simple yet profound belief that everyone deserves a second chance.Not just to survive — but to walk, to move, and to truly live again.\n\nPart II — Hospital Preparedness and Mass Casualty Response\n\n[Approximately 05:17] Interview introduction\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nSo first To explore how hospitals in Lebanon respond to trauma and mass casualty events, I began by speaking with someone who sees it all from the front lines.\n\nDr. George Abi Saad is the Head of Trauma Services, Critical Care Surgery, and the Surgical ICU at the American University of Beirut Medical Center.I spoke with him, about how hospitals in Lebanon prepare  and respond to mass casualty events.\n\n\n\nWhen disaster strikes, trauma teams face enormous pressure. Every second counts.So I asked Dr. Abi Saad: how do hospitals like AUBMC prepare for such emergencies? What happens in those first critical moments after the patients arrive?\n\nHere’s our conversation.\n\n[Approximately 05:57] Interview response — English voice-over\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\nAt the American University of Beirut Medical Center, we place a strong emphasis on trauma preparedness. One of the key tools we rely on is the Advanced Trauma Life Support course — or ATLS. It’s a two-day intensive training program originally developed in the U.S. which I adopted and implemented here at AUBMC. We teach the course to our residents who are specializing with us, as well as to doctors working in the emergency department — to ensure they’re fully prepared to handle trauma cases using a unified and systematic approach. Nurses also take a parallel course, Advanced Trauma Care for Nurses (ATCN),\n\nThese course prepares doctors and nurses to handle any trauma situation — whether the patient has been in a car crash, injured in a bombing, or is suffering from burns, stabbings, or blunt force injuries. The goal is for every doctor and nurse to follow a clear, systematic approach from the moment the patient arrives.\n\nWhen a mass casualty event occurs, we activate our triage system. The ER is divided into four zones:\n\nRed Zone: for patients who need immediate surgery to survive — for example, someone bleeding from the neck.\n\nYellow Zone: for serious injuries that can wait briefly, like abdominal wounds without active bleeding.\n\nGreen Zone: for minor injuries such as glass cuts or superficial shrapnel wounds.\n\nBlack Zone: for those who are already deceased or beyond saving.\n\nEach zone is staffed by a full medical team: an attending physician, a nurse, a resident, and medical students. Triage officers are responsible for deciding which patient goes where, based on the severity of their condition. And when surgery is required, the trauma team calls in the appropriate specialist: orthopedic surgeons for limb injuries, cardiothoracic surgeons for chest trauma, neurosurgeons for brain injuries, and so on.\n\nThey don’t call every doctor. They directly contact the heads of 7–8 critical divisions, who then mobilize their teams.\n\nThis structure ensures that teams work in parallel, not chaos. Without it, you cannot manage hundreds of patients — like the 370 people injured in the Beirut blast. Each team focuses on its zone, and senior leaders, like the heads of the emergency and surgery departments, circulate constantly to monitor everything. Behind all of this is a clearly defined Emergency Preparedness Plan (EPP)\n\n\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\n\n\nBut no plan is ever perfect. I wanted to know — does this strategy change from one crisis to another? After a mass casualty event like the Pager explosion, did the team  find weaknesses in the system? And if so, how do they fix them?\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\nDuring the Pager explosion, one major challenge we faced was imaging. At the time, our ER had only one CT scanner, and we received over 180 patients with shrapnel injuries to the eyes and limbs — all requiring immediate scans. The other two CT scanners in the hospital, located in the main imaging department downstairs, were still operating on a normal schedule for outpatient appointments, so we couldn’t rely on them.\n\nAfter that experience, we revised our emergency protocol. Now, in the event of another mass casualty incident, we plan to immediately cancel all outpatient appointments and clear all three CT scanners — the one in the ER and the two downstairs — so they’re fully dedicated to trauma patients. With this setup, we can triple our imaging capacity in a crisis and avoid the bottlenecks we experienced that day.\n\nA decade ago, we didn’t have ultrasound in the ER, so identifying internal bleeding was difficult. Today, we use the FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) scan — a rapid bedside ultrasound that allows us to quickly detect organ damage and bleeding, helping us make faster, life-saving decisions.Blood readiness was another challenge. In the past, we’d have to wait to test blood types before transfusions — but bleeding patients can’t wait. So we launched a Massive Transfusion Protocol that ensures blood and platelets, including O-negative, are prepared in advance. It’s one of the most important upgrades we’ve made.Each time we find a weakness, we build a solution. For example, we created a task force called FEMEA to study delays between ER admission and surgery. Their goal is to reduce that time from 45 minutes to 15 minutes. It’s a huge effort — but it can mean the difference between saving a life or not.\n\nWe also run regular simulation drills, where we act out a full-scale mass casualty scenario to test our systems. We identify delays — maybe the elevator gets stuck, maybe imaging backs up — and we fix them before the next real emergency.\n\nWe don’t just hope to be ready. We train for it, over and over again.”\n\n[Approximately 10:57] Building a stronger healthcare system\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nHearing how each experience led to concrete improvements thst made me wonder about the bigger picture.Beyond individual hospitals, can Lebanon — after everything it has endured — build a healthcare system that truly supports trauma survivors?” \n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Dr. George Abi Saad\n\n“I believe Lebanon can build an effective healthcare system — but it requires organization and national coordination. It’s not just about how many hospitals or doctors we have; what important  is how we structure and manage the system.\n\nThe Ministry of Health cannot do everything alone. Universities, research centers, and medical institutions need to collaborate. We must conduct field assessments to study hospital readiness — looking at operating rooms, emergency protocols, and equipment — and identify gaps.\n\nThere’s no such thing as 'it can’t be fixed.' Every crisis we’ve faced — from the 2006 war to the Beirut blast — has taught us lessons. During the  2006 war, we performed surgeries underground for 33 days. During the Beirut explosion, hospitals and medical staff were themselves victims — yet we continued to operate.\n\nEach mass casualty event must be treated as an opportunity to improve, not just to recover.\n\nTrauma doesn’t only affect the wounded — it affects families, communities, and society as a whole. One injury can ripple through an entire family’s future.\n\nSo yes, Lebanon can have a strong trauma care system. But it requires smarter planning, stronger emergency preparedness plans like the EPP, faster protocols, and continuous training.\n\nWe don’t wait for the next disaster to learn.We simulate.We adapt.Because every second matters — and every life is worth saving.”\n\n[Approximately 12:57] Transition to prosthetic rehabilitation\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\n\n\nWhat stood out to me in Dr. Abi Saad’s words was not just resilience — but a deep belief in learning from every crisis.Building a stronger healthcare system isn’t just about surviving disasters. It’s about being ready, staying ready, and protecting every life — no matter the circumstances.\n\nBut survival is only the beginning. For many patients, the real journey starts after the emergency — when they must learn to live with an amputation and navigate the long road of physical and emotional recovery.\n\nTo better understand what happens after the trauma, I spoke with Specialist Bassam Singer, the owner of Center Singer for Prosthetics and Orthotics.We talked about the challenges of accessing prosthetic care in Lebanon, the technologies that are available, and the deep personal impact of losing — and regaining — mobility.\n\n\n\nPart III — Prosthetics, Rehabilitation, and Access to Care\n\n[Approximately 14:27] Prosthetics and orthotics\n\nDr. Singer began by explaining the foundation of his work — and the difference between prosthetics and orthotics.\n\nAt our center, we specialize in two main areas: prosthetics, which deal with amputations, and orthotics, which support patients with conditions like scoliosis, paralysis, or neuromuscular disorders. In those cases, we create customized orthopedic devices to help with posture, mobility, and muscle compensation.\n\nBut prosthetics and orthotics alone are not enough. Our work always includes physiotherapy because every device must be paired with physical preparation and retraining. Before fitting a prosthetic, we begin strengthening the body, improving balance, and teaching the patient how to adapt. This applies to every patient, but especially to children with conditions like cerebral palsy. \n\nFrom a technology standpoint, we’re fully up to date. We use CAD-CAM systems and 3D scanning to create customized devices based on the patient's exact measurements. For upper limbs, we use myoelectric prosthetics. We place electrodes on the muscles, and when the brain sends a signal, like “open hand” — the prosthetic responds. in some casesb If the nerves in the arm stump are too weak, we reroute signals from a stronger area — like the shoulder — to restore control.\n\nFor lower limbs, we use microprocessor-controlled prosthetics. These are programmed through dedicated software and can even be adjusted using a mobile app. \n\nThese technologies give us powerful tools to rebuild motion.\n\nWe also offer aesthetic prosthetics in special cases. After the Pager explosion, for example, we treated a patient who had lost several fingers. In those situations, we create silicone replicas that match the original hand in shape, color, texture, and even hair. These prosthetics aren’t functional — they don’t move — but they restore confidence and visual normalcy.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nDr. Singer’s team is clearly at the cutting edge — but not every patient has access to that level of care. So I asked him about the cost of prosthetics in Lebanon, and who can actually afford them.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\nThe price of a prosthetic depends on many factors — the patient’s age, physical condition, lifestyle, and whether they need something basic or advanced. Devices can range from $1,500 Up to one hundred ten thousand U.S. dollars $110,000.\n\nIf a 70-year-old patient needs a leg just to get to the bathroom, we recommend something simple. But if a young, active person comes in — and they can afford it — we fit them with a smart prosthetic. If not, we offer a basic, mechanical one that still meets essential needs.\n\nFor children, it’s more complicated. They outgrow their prosthetics quickly. The socket needs to be replaced every three months. The entire limb — every six months. That makes pediatric care much more expensive, even if we don’t use advanced technology.\n\nAnd unfortunately, there’s no governmental support. The Lebanese Army is the only exception, and even their budget is extremely limited now. Social Security and the Ministry of Health don’t classify prosthetics as essential. They treat it as a secondary or non-essential service—what they often call an “extra” or add-on—so they don’t allocate budget for it.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nDespite all the technology and skill, Dr. Singer emphasized that the most powerful part of his work isn’t mechanical — it’s emotional.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\nA prosthetic isn’t just a medical device. It’s a process — and it’s deeply emotional. Many patients arrive at our center in wheelchairs, emotionally devastated. But when they finally stand up, walk, or regain a small motion — the moment is overwhelming. I’ve seen people cry, laugh, freeze in disbelief. It’s the moment they realize their life is not over.\n\nBut getting to that point isn’t easy. Rehabilitation is the real challenge. The first stage is pre-fitting physiotherapy. We help the patient rebuild strength, balance, and coordination — preparing them to support themselves. Only after this phase do we start training with the prosthetic.\n\nThe entire adaptation process typically takes one week to a month, depending on the person. Most patients visit us three to four times per week, with each session lasting up to four hours. Every case is different, and progress depends on physical strength, psychological readiness, and personal motivation.\n\nFor children, the process is even more demanding. Their bodies are still growing, which means more frequent changes — and more physical and emotional support. But the impact of helping a child walk again? It’s something you never forget.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nAs our conversation came to a close, I asked Dr. Singer to look ahead — not just at the next patient walking through his door, but at the bigger picture. What does the future hold for prosthetic care in Lebanon, a country full of talent but short on support?\n\nHawraa Ayoub — English voice-over of Bassam Singer\n\n\\\n\nThe technology is here. We stay up to date with every advancement. At our center, we follow the global innovation in prosthetics — we use scanning, software, smart systems. We’re ready to lead.\n\nAnd we have the talent. Lebanon has students, engineers, and specialists with incredible potential. I’ve worked with young professionals who could easily compete internationally — not just in fitting prosthetics, but in designing and building them. From a human resources perspective, we’re not behind.\n\nBut even with all our technology and talent, we’re still working within the limitations of a country facing deep economic and structural challenges. Lebanon is a resilient nation — but it’s also a fragile one. And that’s why, today, there’s still no national policy or sustainable funding to support prosthetic care.\n\nIn many countries, the healthcare system ensures that prosthetics are fully covered. Here, patients often have to navigate the process on their own. The public sector does not yet treat prosthetics as a guaranteed right — and that leaves many people behind.\n\nAt the same time, we’re doing what we can with what we have. While we currently import all the electronic components, joints, and materials, we do manufacture the socket locally — the part that connects the prosthetic to the patient’s body. It’s a start. And it’s a sign that with the right investment and coordination, Lebanon could one day expand its local production — reducing costs, increasing access, and becoming a regional leader in prosthetic innovation.\n\nPart IV — Closing Reflections\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nListening to Dr. Singer, one thing becomes clear: even in a fragile system, progress is possible. Piece by piece, socket by socket, a future is being built — not just in technology, but in determination.\n\nProsthetics in Lebanon may still depend on imported parts, but the spirit behind them is entirely local — driven by doctors, engineers, physiotherapists, and patients who refuse to give up.\n\nAs I wrap up this podcast, I find myself thinking less about endings, and more about continuations.\n\nBecause stories like these don’t stop when the patient is discharged. They don’t stop at the prosthetic fitting, or the physical therapy session. They continue — in every step taken, every skill relearned, and every system quietly being improved by those who believe that mobility is a right, not a privilege.\n\nWhat I’ve learned in this journey so far is that Lebanon is not lacking in brilliance. It’s not lacking in drive, in expertise, or in compassion. What it needs is coordination. Vision. And the courage to treat recovery as a collective responsibility.\n\nAnd I’m not done asking questions.\n\nIn the coming weeks, I’ll be continuing this project — not through a podcast series, but through deeper conversations and expanded research. I’ve already scheduled an interview with Dr. Jamal Hoballah, Chairperson and Professor of Surgery at AUBMC. With his expertise in vascular surgery, stents, and reperfusion injury, I want to better understand the decision-making behind amputations — and how the body, and the system, can prepare for healing before the wound even closes.\n\nI’m also planning to reach out to the Center for the Protection of the Rights of the Disabled — to explore the legal, social, and emotional landscapes that shape the lives of amputees after they leave the hospital. What protections exist under Lebanese law? What barriers — financial, infrastructural, or cultural — still stand in the way of full reintegration? I want to understand how issues like health insurance coverage, employment rights, access to public space, and the cost of long-term prosthetic maintenance affect people’s ability not just to survive, but to truly participate in society. Because recovery doesn’t end with physical healing. It continues in the systems that either support or neglect those trying to rebuild their lives.\n\nThis project began with a question about prosthetics. But what it’s really about — is people. Systems. And the silent strength it takes to rebuild not just a limb, but a life.\n\nThank you for listening. And thank you for walking this path with me.\n\nSupplementary Passage — Present in the Supplied Script\n\nThe following passage appears after the closing thanks in the supplied text. Its position in the actual recording is unverified, so it is retained separately rather than assigned an invented timestamp.\n\nHawraa Ayoub — Host\n\nSo even though Lebanon could become a hub for innovation in prosthetics, we’re blocked by economic collapse and lack of government investment. Until that changes, prosthetic care will remain a privilege — not a standard of care.\n\nAs our conversation drew to a close, one thing became clear: Lebanon doesn’t lack knowledge, or skill, or ambition.It lacks structure.It lacks support.And in the world of prosthetics, that gap doesn’t just mean a delayed treatment — it means someone’s future put on hold.\n\nThe technology exists. The talent is here. But without public investment and national policy, prosthetic care will remain a silent crisis — one lived by those who are already carrying more than enough loss.\n\nDr. Singer’s words reminded me that innovation without access is just decoration. And in Lebanon today, mobility — like so much else — too often depends on privilege, not need.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n", "credits": "Hawraa Ayoub\n\nPodcast creator and researcher\n\nHost and narrator\n\nInterviewer\n\nScriptwriter and English voice-over narrator\n\nCover image creator (AI-assisted)\n\nDr. George Abi Saad\n\nInterviewee\n\nTrauma surgeon at the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC)\n\nContributed expertise on hospital preparedness, emergency response, and mass casualty management\n\nDr. Bassam Singer\n\nInterviewee\n\nProsthetics and orthotics specialist, Center Singer\n\nContributed expertise on prosthetic technologies, accessibility, costs, and rehabilitation in Lebanon\n\nInterview with Dr. George Abi Saad, Trauma Surgeon at AUBMC. Interview conducted in Arabic by Hawraa Ayoub; English voice-over by Hawraa Ayoub.\n\nInterview with Bassam Singer, Prosthetics and Orthotics Specialist at Center Singer. Interview conducted in Arabic by Hawraa Ayoub; English voice-over by Hawraa Ayoub.", "contentNote": ""});

// Podcast sequence requested by curator.
{const works=window.EXHIBITION.works;const order=['the-body-remembers','rebuilt-lives','negotiated-care','war-medicine'];window.EXHIBITION.works=[...works.filter(w=>w.type!=='podcast'),...order.map(id=>works.find(w=>w.id===id))];}

window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="negotiated-care").statement="I chose this topic specifically from a curiosity to explore how far can personal connections be a deterministic factor in a sector governed by ministries, hospitals and insurance companies; so in a system where a logic exists at least on paper. After being myself a victim of personal connections, not in the medical sector specifically, yet in a system that closely parallels it. I was trying to bridge the gap between personal circumstances and actual lived experience in meritocratic systems.\n\nThe main issue of this gap is that it is regularly found in nearly every encounter with people talking about their treatment plans. On paper, we find many coverage systems through the Ministry of Public Health or the National Social Security Fund or private schemes, but in reality, this can never guarantee full treatment. Moreover, nearly every time, factors outside the formally recognized systems such as the ability to take advantage of personal sectarian-based connections, become decisive throughout this process. To address this issue I conducted the three interviews previously described.\n\nTaken together these insights generate a clear thesis: The Lebanese healthcare system in not a merely neutral system, instead, it is negotiated across many layers, political, financial and social. The political layer represents the first negotiation panel translated by a fragmented system with a scattered decision-making system. Second, the negotiation moves into the financial layer with inconsistent coverage and economic instability constantly reshaping it. Third, when this gap is enlarged between formal eligibility and reality, the negotiation resides in informal mechanisms; a type of secret gateway representing the last grain of hope for a large portion of the Lebanese population. Therefore, wasta is not just a side-effect of this issue in healthcare system, it becomes a primary decisive factor compensating for existing constraints. It functions at the cost of fairness and promotes a new type of inequality depending from which sect the negotiation happens instead of eradicating it.\n\nUltimately, Negotiated care calls for a change in how the healthcare system should be understood. A holistic comprehension of the system should be a combination of formal as well as informal routes for access. Then, it would be possible to understand that expanding services or coverage is not a solution instead, a remodeling of the system itself from welfare to proper medical service would be a plausible approach.";

window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="the-body-remembers").transcriptCues=[{"time": 0.46, "label": "0:00", "text": "Host: [music] What if the body never forgets what the mind tries so hard to bury?"}, {"time": 10.76, "label": "0:10", "text": "Welcome to my podcast, The Body Remembers. This podcast is about how trauma lives not just in the mind, but in the body. In a place like Lebanon, where war has become generational, that truth becomes undeniable."}, {"time": 25.58, "label": "0:25", "text": "In September 2024, Lebanon found itself once again engulfed in violence. The conflict that began earlier in late 2023 on the southern borders rapidly spread into populated areas, sparked by a pager attack, displacing thousands and claiming multitudes of civilian lives. As of December, the Ministry of Public Health of Lebanon has reported 4,047 deaths and more than 16,000 injuries. Yet, the full toll of the war is still being counted—not in the bodies, but in minds and lives left in psychological ruin."}, {"time": 65.22, "label": "1:05", "text": "Host: While the world turns its attention to casualty numbers and geopolitical strategies, far less attention is paid to the silent wounds. The body, we know now with advances in psychology and psychiatry, remembers trauma in ways that are just as persistent and devastating as psychological symptoms."}, {"time": 85.5, "label": "1:25", "text": "However, most reports and articles that I found on the internet or databases—including those from Global Health, UNICEF, and The New Arab, to list a few—focus primarily on physical casualties or reduce the psychological toll of war to a single diagnosis like PTSD. They often treat somatic symptoms and mental health issues as parallel outcomes of the war, as if they are mutually exclusive rather than deeply interconnected."}, {"time": 115.28, "label": "1:55", "text": "Some may dismiss this claim, saying that not enough time has passed since the war for studies to be published. Yes, I agree. This, however, is not just about the 2024 war. Lebanon's modern history is punctuated by war-induced or similar traumas: the Civil War from 1975 to 1990, the 2006 war with Israel, and the Beirut explosion of 2020, which is the biggest non-nuclear explosion of the modern era. Each of these has left not only visible destruction, but also invisible legacies of pain."}, {"time": 153.44, "label": "2:33", "text": "Host: I reviewed a great number of studies regarding these tragedies, and the trend was the same. Most discuss the psychological impact and somatic health separately. When the literature does talk about mental health after the war, they reduce the conversation to numbers: 47.8% of Lebanese youth now suffer from anxiety; PTSD prevalence rose by 30% post-2020; and so on, without elaboration on the somatic experience. When they do refer to them, like in a study by Dr. Laila Farhood, they simply list the generic physical symptoms of PTSD that match with the DSM-5's definition of PTSD, like sleep disturbances and heartbeat acceleration."}, {"time": 202.0, "label": "3:22", "text": "This is not at all what I've seen as a person who went through the recent war myself. The reality scars that the war left are far more persistent and complex than simple percentages of PTSD. The interplay between body and mind became ever more multifaceted when it came to the war. The way people suffered from the war was each a unique story of their own, but these stories are sadly reduced to like 0.01 percentage of another PTSD case."}, {"time": 235.36, "label": "3:55", "text": "Host: This podcast, titled The Body Remembers, is about that interplay between the mind and the body, between what we can name and what we cannot. It's about how the pain of war manifests somatically in ways we often miss. To do so, I will examine the oral histories of the victims of the war, the story that I argue goes missing in academia."}, {"time": 261.2, "label": "4:21", "text": "There will be two interviews, each of which is around eight minutes. Although the actual interview was longer, for the time being, I deeply regret having cut off some of what was said. My approach to the two interviews was rather different. The first interview was with a close friend of mine who has gone through a lot during the war. With her, I purposely limited my questions as much as I could. It was rather a conversational format where I allowed the interviewee to reflect on herself and speak freely of her experience."}, {"time": 298.72, "label": "4:58", "text": "There were moments when the interviewee got too emotional, and we had to halt the interview for a while. On the contrary, the second interview was conducted with a set of questions as it was with a scholar of our topic."}, {"time": 312.98, "label": "5:12", "text": "So let's now hear more from our interviewees, starting with our speaker, Dalaa Zaarour, a student from American University of Beirut."}, {"time": 321.32, "label": "5:21", "text": "Host: Hello."}, {"time": 323.42, "label": "5:23", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: Hello."}, {"time": 336.24, "label": "5:36", "text": "Host: Can you briefly introduce yourself?"}, {"time": 338.12, "label": "5:38", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: My name is Dalaa Zaarour. I'm 20 years old, and I'm a bio major in AUB. I'm Lebanese-Palestinian."}, {"time": 344.98, "label": "5:44", "text": "Host: Can you tell us about what happened to you during the war in Lebanon?"}, {"time": 350.08, "label": "5:50", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: Okay, so the war started in September 2024. September 23, they started bombing the south of Lebanon, and it was so like—all of my family lives there except for me and my parents and my brothers. So it was so stressing, and I was in uni, and I was like panicking a lot. And I was calling my family, extended family, and no one was answering, and no one had like connection. So yeah."}, {"time": 388.16, "label": "6:28", "text": "And then, like the next day—I think the same or the next day—they hit where I live. Me and my parents, we grabbed anything we could, and then we went to Jabal, to Aley, and we stayed there."}, {"time": 410.14, "label": "6:50", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: First, we couldn't find a place to stay, so we went to different destinations. So first we went to different destinations in the district, in Aley district, until we settled in a place called Mtein or something—Mtein. So yeah."}, {"time": 429.2, "label": "7:09", "text": "So this was the first two days of the war. After that, like when we settled and we kept on going back to Beirut to get our stuff up until until we received the news that they bombed Zuqaq al-Blat. And sadly, some of my family members were there, and some of them sadly passed away."}, {"time": 458.02, "label": "7:38", "text": "So after that, like towards the end of the war, we received news that our house got destroyed, our building. And then the ceasefire was announced. Okay, and then me and my family stayed a bit in Aley, and then we went back, and we found a new place to settle in."}, {"time": 481.88, "label": "8:01", "text": "Host: I know it's not easy to reflect and share this kind of experiences, but could you share more and elaborate more on how you felt during the war, what kind of things you went through, and how were people around you? Did they go through similar stuff like you?"}, {"time": 498.28, "label": "8:18", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: So during the war, my family and my extended family, we were all distributed across all parts of Lebanon. And of course, everyone was in danger because there were so many places that were getting bombed. And as you know, uni was hybrid, so I had to go to Beirut for some time so I could attend classes in uni because some professors required us to do so."}, {"time": 532.2, "label": "8:52", "text": "But at the same time, my parents were in a different part of Lebanon; they were in Jabal. So I was so anxious all the time, and I was so stressed and scared that something would happen to them, and they might get hurt, or they might get bombed or something."}, {"time": 553.38, "label": "9:13", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: And this reflected on my physical health as well. I had no appetite, and my parents were so worried to the point—I used to live with my aunt in Beirut. So my parents were so worried after she told them, so I was rushed to the ER, and they had to give me IV bags. They had to give me IV bags."}, {"time": 576.12, "label": "9:36", "text": "And so after that, I went back home, and my physical health was still in a very weak state. I started feeling numbness in the left side of my body, shortness of breath, basically the symptoms that would feel like you are having a heart attack. So I was rushed to the ER again for a second time, and the doctor that was present in the ER told me that my body was faking a heart attack due to severe trauma from the war."}, {"time": 616.16, "label": "10:16", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: And then I was referred to a psychiatrist, but I thought about it, and I didn't want to make my parents worry, so I was reluctant to go. So even though I knew that visiting a psychiatrist would help me a lot, I did not go because often people in our society—people who visit the psychiatrists in our society are stigmatized and often stereotyped as weird people."}, {"time": 644.14, "label": "10:44", "text": "So after the ceasefire, I thought my life was going to go back to normal, but it appeared that my body had been severely affected, and my mental state had been severely affected by this war. So anytime anyone would talk loudly, or someone would close the door and it would make a loud noise, or whenever I'm driving around and someone beeps at me, I would get startled, I'd get anxious, my heart rate starts racing as if it's going to jump out of my chest, and it starts squeezing as well."}, {"time": 682.54, "label": "11:22", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: So before the war, I used to have really strong immunity, and whenever I used to get sick, it would only last for about like two or three days. But after the ceasefire, I've caught the flu like three times, and I had severe symptoms. They were very strong, so I was hospitalized."}, {"time": 703.84, "label": "11:43", "text": "As for people around me, some of them did go through the same things that I went through, especially my family and my cousins. But this wasn't the case for my foreigner friends or my uni friends that have lived abroad. Although they went through displacement and so on, after the ceasefire, they seemed fine. Like, they seemed okay."}, {"time": 725.18, "label": "12:05", "text": "Dalaa Zaarour: Maybe because my family and I, as Lebanese-Palestinians, we went through a lot. So my parents have lived through almost every single war that happened in Lebanon, be it the Civil War, the 1982 war, the 2006, and as well as the recent one in 2024. And my grandparents are victims of the Nakba in 1948."}, {"time": 756.14, "label": "12:36", "text": "And I remember when we were kids, my grandma used to gather us around and tell us stories from that period of time. I remember she told us that they didn't even have time to gather essential belongings, so they just fled on foot from Palestine to the borders."}, {"time": 773.3, "label": "12:53", "text": "So yes, maybe those reoccurring senses of fear caused me and my family to suffer more physically and mentally from the war."}, {"time": 787.94, "label": "13:07", "text": "Host: [music] It is truly not easy to listen to those stories of pain, especially of your close ones. But I believe it is through these stories that we advance in understanding of our body and mind, so as to provide correct support for these victims."}, {"time": 809.2, "label": "13:29", "text": "Next, we will be hearing from a clinical psychologist in the AUBMC, Ms. Charlotte Al-Khalil, who has been working closely with the displaced people in Lebanon since the onset of the war."}, {"time": 823.64, "label": "13:43", "text": "Host: Can you introduce yourself very quickly?"}, {"time": 831.9, "label": "13:51", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: Yeah. So I'm Charlotte Khalil, psychologist. I work here at the American University of Beirut Medical Center, and I was leading the initiative of the department. We were providing mental health support for the displaced in two shelters here around AUB."}, {"time": 850.26, "label": "14:10", "text": "Host: So can you please share your personal encounters with patients who have suffered from war psychologically, and how this psychological impact has manifested physically?"}, {"time": 861.16, "label": "14:21", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: In my work with patients affected by war and trauma in general, I can say that trauma isn't just a story we tell or even a memory we hold. It lives in the body. That's what we know. So many patients present with physical symptoms that seem to have no clear medical cause, like chronic pain, like sleep problems, fatigue, muscle pain."}, {"time": 885.46, "label": "14:45", "text": "And during the war, I would like to give an example here. During the war, a client presented with chest tightness. Every time a loud noise occurred, she used to feel this pain. Medically, everything was okay. Nothing wrong with her heart, her body. But what we realized after starting therapy is that her body had learned to associate sound with danger."}, {"time": 915.1, "label": "15:15", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: And we know that the body keeps the score and the body will react. So react through muscular problems with like any kind of physical reaction. That's why when we implemented therapy, she noticed a reducing in the like physical symptoms."}, {"time": 936.06, "label": "15:36", "text": "And you asked me about my clinical work, so I witnessed firsthand how psychological trauma often, even when we are not aware of the impact—because you can encounter people, especially here in Lebanon, we have a lot of defense mechanisms. So, \"We are okay. We don't—we are not afraid,\" you know? And at the end, we react differently. What we cannot tell and express verbally, our body will tell this way."}, {"time": 967.08, "label": "16:07", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: So I think I can relate both in this way. And I would like also to give an example about an adolescent, because when we talk about psychosomatic, we think that it's just related to adults. That's not true. We witness it within kids, within adolescents."}, {"time": 985.64, "label": "16:25", "text": "A 13-year-old boy came to therapy for severe stomach pain. The parents did all the medical exams and they didn't find anything. And after therapy, it was really related to the bombing and what they had to endure as a family during the war."}, {"time": 1005.08, "label": "16:45", "text": "So I think it's there. The connection is there. The mind and body are very well connected, and we can tell here that the body becomes a silent carrier of unprocessed fear and loss."}, {"time": 1023.6, "label": "17:03", "text": "Host: As a psychologist, what is your opinion on how PTSD or similar mental illness in the context of war affect physical health?"}, {"time": 1033.46, "label": "17:13", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: So trauma literally reshapes both brain and body. That's what we know about it. It disrupts the nervous system, affects the hormone levels, and keeps individuals in a state of hyperarousal or even numbness, so it depends on the reaction. This can lead to long-term physical health issues. We can, I think, see cardiovascular disease, autoimmune conditions, chronic pain like we mentioned before."}, {"time": 1064.4, "label": "17:44", "text": "And what we know that feeling safe is a main need for human beings. So when they don't feel safe and inside their own skin, they will react. That's why I do believe that addressing mental health also requires helping people reconnect with their bodies in a safe way and in a safe environment. And that's what is very challenging when you do intervention in a country like Lebanon or in a war setting."}, {"time": 1101.24, "label": "18:21", "text": "Host: Thank you. So as a scholar in the field of clinical psychology, do you think this connection between body and mental illness and the complex physical symptoms beyond PTSD is done enough, or are they quite limited?"}, {"time": 1115.06, "label": "18:35", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: I do believe that we need a lot of work to be done on this subject because studies are like—okay, we know that when we describe symptoms, when we talk about trauma and PTSD, we describe a lot of symptoms related to physical reactions, but it's not enough. I think we should combine both and control for both."}, {"time": 1139.78, "label": "18:59", "text": "And we need a lot of work on this to be able to understand this connection, not just to discover it—we know it's there—but maybe to tailor intervention and, you know, to also guide our intervention as psychologists and even as, you know, physicians."}, {"time": 1166.28, "label": "19:26", "text": "Host: Yes. Thank you."}, {"time": 1168.32, "label": "19:28", "text": "Charlotte Al-Khalil: So I think it's very important to address the body's response to trauma because if we want to support people and communities affected by war, we need to integrate body-based and trauma-informed approaches both together. So ignoring the somatic experience of trauma limits our ability to truly help, I think, because we will miss a lot of details there."}, {"time": 1193.44, "label": "19:53", "text": "And healing involves restoring a sense of control, reconnecting with the body, and building secure connections with others. So it's a whole intervention that we should implement to be able to really do an effective intervention. And to do that, we need a lot of studies. And to do this, I think a collaboration is needed between different departments to be able to find the correct response to that."}, {"time": 1222.18, "label": "20:22", "text": "Host: Okay. Thank you so much."}, {"time": 1231.9, "label": "20:31", "text": "Host: What we've just heard reinforces a difficult truth: the body becomes a silent carrier of unprocessed fear, and the somatic manifestation of those triggers become very complex in the context of war."}, {"time": 1250.84, "label": "20:50", "text": "And I must mention that the stories we've heard today in our podcast are only the tip of an iceberg. I volunteered in many events that aided the displaced people during the war, especially in medical aids, and I've seen a lot more—from a girl in her teens suffering from sudden urination, to a young woman losing almost all her hair."}, {"time": 1273.78, "label": "21:13", "text": "Yet in most cases, the unique experiences of patients are simply classified under PTSD using rigid, top-down framework, usually by matching a patient's symptoms to a checklist in the DSM. Perhaps this is another example of drawbacks of medicalization, where we try to fit every illness experience into diagnostic names written in the guideline like in the DSM-5, regardless of the complexity and diversity of illness experienced by the victims of the war."}, {"time": 1304.04, "label": "21:44", "text": "Host: According to the DSM-5, PTSD is defined as exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, followed by symptoms such as intrusive thoughts, negative mood, sleep dysfunction, or hyperarousal. But what if your symptoms are different? What if your trauma expresses through irritable bowel syndrome, immune dysfunction, or chronic chest pain, like the example given by Mrs. Charlotte Khalil?"}, {"time": 1332.58, "label": "22:12", "text": "Let's consider a case of Gulf War syndrome. After the 1991 Gulf War, thousands of veterans reported a range of symptoms that included memory loss, joint pain, rashes, and chronic fatigue. For years, their complaints were dismissed, but today, the US Department of Veterans Affairs recognizes Gulf War syndrome as a legitimate multi-symptom illness. This shows how war can alter the body in profound and unpredictable ways that do not necessarily match with the existing classifications in the DSM."}, {"time": 1368.3, "label": "22:48", "text": "Host: Without this understanding, we cannot provide pertinent intervention and, to borrow what Ms. Khalil said, not just to discover, but to tailor intervention. We need more studies, especially in the region of constant violence like in Lebanon, not just in psychology, but also in the field of medicine and biology."}, {"time": 1388.38, "label": "23:08", "text": "And this brings us to a critical field of research: epigenetics and sociogenomics. Epigenetics refers to a study of how environmental factors, including trauma, can change gene expression. According to a 2009 study by Dr. Yehuda and his colleagues, trauma survivors showed epigenetic changes related to stress hormone regulation, particularly in genes that affect the amygdala and HPA axis, which is the biological stress response system. It is not a single study; multiple studies done on different trauma experiences, such as 9/11 terror or Holocaust or personal trauma experiences, show the same results."}, {"time": 1430.18, "label": "23:50", "text": "Host: Even more shockingly, some of these epigenetic changes appear to be inheritable, according to a study done by Dr. Dias in 2013 and by Dr. Mulligan and his colleagues in their study on Syrian refugees."}, {"time": 1446.24, "label": "24:06", "text": "The sociogenomic model takes this one step further. It suggests that trauma-based epigenetic changes can influence how entire communities respond to stress. According to Dalton Conley, the author of The Social Genome: The New Science of Nature and Nurture, these inherited markers can feed back into cultural narratives, shaping behaviors, identities, and even further risk of psychiatric illness. This is a loop effect: trauma reinforcing trauma biologically and socially."}, {"time": 1477.6, "label": "24:37", "text": "Perhaps this is related to what our first interviewee, Tala, said about how her family lineage saturated with war could be the culprit that impacted her and her family significantly more than others. This is especially alarming because it goes against what is commonly believed—that people become immune to trauma; those who survive the war will be more numb and okay with experiencing another one, as they have already gone through one. However, it seems that it is the exact opposite."}, {"time": 1509.84, "label": "25:09", "text": "Host: What is promising, however, is that our expanded knowledge in epigenetics and sociogenomics can be a power, as Dalton Conley says in his essay. A study done by Dr. Anne Brunet and his colleagues on animals showed that epigenetic changes are reversible. Also, the understanding of sociogenomics can guide for how we treat patients in the region of Lebanon, helping to create appropriate, safe environments for victims of the war."}, {"time": 1542.06, "label": "25:42", "text": "Unfortunately, a lot of obstacles must first be addressed to further such research and investigation in Lebanon. The first is the embedded stigma in Lebanese society. Our first interviewee, Tala, explicitly stated that she refused to visit a psychiatrist due to the stigma towards people who visit the psychiatry department. And this barrier is also well-documented in many studies. In 2022, Dr. Abi Doumit released her study that said that stigma is the number one factor that makes patients reluctant to visit psychiatrists, and this is a clear obstacle towards this kind of research. The same is for many other Arab countries due to deeply tied, conservative religious beliefs of the region, according to Dr. Zeinoun."}, {"time": 1594.16, "label": "26:34", "text": "Host: Also, as a resident here in Lebanon, it seems that the silence of academia is rationalized by the frequency and commonness of the war scar, where people say everyone experiences it, everyone goes through it, it's not a big of a deal. That is why we need an interdisciplinary endeavor, not just in psychology or biology, but also in social dimensions."}, {"time": 1616.72, "label": "26:56", "text": "If the body remembers, then we must listen. The somatic effects of war trauma, from chronic pain to epigenetic inheritance, are not marginal symptoms. They're central to lived experience of survivors. We cannot simply take a top-down approach in reducing these sufferings as mere symptoms of another PTSD. Treating them requires more than just medication or talk therapy. It demands a whole-body approach, one that honors the unique expressions and experiences of trauma across individuals and communities."}, {"time": 1654.38, "label": "27:34", "text": "In Lebanon, the burden is especially heavy—not just because of the frequency of wars, but because of the silence that surrounds its aftermath. We must invest in trauma-informed care, in culturally competent research, and in an interdisciplinary healing practices that bridge psychiatry, neuroscience, and social policy. We cannot afford to ignore what our body remembers."}, {"time": 1683.8, "label": "28:03", "text": "Host: And with that, I would like to end with some personal remarks on this podcast. Preparing this podcast meant a lot to me, as it was a part of my experience. I was also affected by the war, as my family and my house were very near the bombed areas of Lebanon."}, {"time": 1712.68, "label": "28:32", "text": "Although it was edited off, each interview was accompanied by a personal conversation of mine with the interviewees, sharing my experiences and their experiences in a deeper level. This helped me to reflect back on my experience and bring peaceful period to my traumatic experiences."}, {"time": 1731.16, "label": "28:51", "text": "In addition, as a pre-med student, I hope to bring this lesson of seeing patients in whatever context as unique stories to my future practice. Although I have argued that very limited studies are done on interplay between the body and mind in the context of war, I was very delighted to meet Ms. Charlotte Khalil, who shared the same critical view on field and was actually working on a research paper on this topic."}, {"time": 1755.68, "label": "29:15", "text": "Lastly, I thank Dr. Joelle Abi-Rached for providing this great opportunity to prepare this podcast. Just like my title, The Body Remembers, my body will remember this course and this podcast."}, {"time": 1768.92, "label": "29:28", "text": "Thank you for listening."}];

window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="rebuilt-lives").transcriptCues=[{"time": 0.64, "label": "0:00", "text": "There are the stories we tell during war — and the stories we forget to tell after."}, {"time": 7.36, "label": "0:07", "text": "The headlines often speak in numbers: airstrikes, casualties, destruction.But they rarely speak of silence — of the long, invisible struggle that begins when the bombing ends."}, {"time": 21.7, "label": "0:21", "text": "That silence is what drew me to this project.My name is Hawraa Ayoub. I’m a chemistry pre-med student at the American University of Beirut, and I’ve always been deeply interested in the intersection between prosthetics and medicine — how science can restore not just limbs, but lives.This project attempt to explore that intersection, and to tell the stories of survival, healing, and hope."}, {"time": 52.96, "label": "0:52", "text": "This project is about that silence. About the journey that starts after survival—about the hospitals that must respond in chaos, and the survivors who must rebuild their lives with prosthetics when the war fades from the news."}, {"time": 70.76, "label": "1:10", "text": "In Lebanon, this story is heartbreakingly familiar.From the 2006 war, to the 2020 Beirut port explosion, and most recently the 2024 pager attack that injured almost 3,000 people, conflict continues to carve deep marks into the bodies and lives of civilians. In the chaos of mass casualty events, emergency rooms fill, surgeries begin, and somewhere along the way, decisions are made—some lives are saved, and some limbs are not."}, {"time": 108.24, "label": "1:48", "text": "According to Reem Karami and Jamal Hoballah in their chapter Amputations and Prostheses, 70% of war injuries affect the musculoskeletal system, particularly the limbs. For many of these patients, amputation is not a last resort—it’s a lifesaving decision. In battlefield conditions, it's often the quickest path to stability, especially when faced with complex blast injuries. But the story doesn’t end with amputation. In many ways, that’s where it begins."}, {"time": 148.28, "label": "2:28", "text": "My project focuses on two critical stages:first, the preparedness of hospitals during trauma injuries, and second, the long road of post-war rehabilitation using prosthetics."}, {"time": 165.04, "label": "2:45", "text": "Hospital readiness is often the line between life and death.When hospitals like LAUMC-RH received dozens of victims after the 2024 pager explosion, 33 out of 38 patients needed immediate surgery, according to Joelle Kalaji and her colleagues.But emergency care is only one half of the story.The other half is what happens after: when patients leave the operating room missing a part of themselves, and must face a long and costly recovery."}, {"time": 208.22, "label": "3:28", "text": "Over the years, as Nour Aoun and Maryam Tajvar (2024) show, Lebanon’s healthcare system has become more and more privatized, meaning that private hospitals and private payments now dominate. Today, over 85% of healthcare costs must be paid directly by patients, making treatment and prosthetics far too expensive for most people."}, {"time": 237.84, "label": "3:57", "text": "Yet, history tells us something powerful. As Robert Gailey (2007) explains, wars have driven innovation in prosthetics — from wooden limbs to today’s brain-controlled neuroprosthetics. But while the technology has advanced, its production remains unequal. In places like Lebanon, prosthetic devices are available, but they’re almost entirely imported. Local manufacturing is extremely limited, making access costly and dependent on external supply chains."}, {"time": 274.8, "label": "4:34", "text": "This project studies that gap — the space between emergency response and long-term healing.It asks: how do we prepare hospitals for mass trauma? And more importantly, do we treat prosthetics as a basic human right... or as a luxury only a few can afford?"}, {"time": 296.18, "label": "4:56", "text": "It’s about systems that break — and people who don’t.It’s about trauma, and it’s about technology.But above all, it’s about the simple yet profound belief that everyone deserves a second chance.Not just to survive — but to walk, to move, and to truly live again."}, {"time": 316.5, "label": "5:16", "text": "So first To explore how hospitals in Lebanon respond to trauma and mass casualty events, I began by speaking with someone who sees it all from the front lines."}, {"time": 328.38, "label": "5:28", "text": "Dr. George Abi Saad is the Head of Trauma Services, Critical Care Surgery, and the Surgical ICU at the American University of Beirut Medical Center.I spoke with him, about how hospitals in Lebanon prepare  and respond to mass casualty events."}, {"time": 347.38, "label": "5:47", "text": "When disaster strikes, trauma teams face enormous pressure. Every second counts.So I asked Dr. Abi Saad: how do hospitals like AUBMC prepare for such emergencies? What happens in those first critical moments after the patients arrive?"}, {"time": 367.52, "label": "6:07", "text": "Here’s our conversation."}, {"time": 370.42, "label": "6:10", "text": "At the American University of Beirut Medical Center, we place a strong emphasis on trauma preparedness. One of the key tools we rely on is the Advanced Trauma Life Support course — or ATLS. It’s a two-day intensive training program originally developed in the U.S. which I adopted and implemented here at AUBMC. We teach the course to our residents who are specializing with us, as well as to doctors working in the emergency department — to ensure they’re fully prepared to handle trauma cases using a unified and systematic approach. Nurses also take a parallel course, Advanced Trauma Care for Nurses (ATCN),"}, {"time": 417.34, "label": "6:57", "text": "These course prepares doctors and nurses to handle any trauma situation — whether the patient has been in a car crash, injured in a bombing, or is suffering from burns, stabbings, or blunt force injuries. The goal is for every doctor and nurse to follow a clear, systematic approach from the moment the patient arrives."}, {"time": 438.3, "label": "7:18", "text": "When a mass casualty event occurs, we activate our triage system. The ER is divided into four zones:"}, {"time": 447.22, "label": "7:27", "text": "Red Zone: for patients who need immediate surgery to survive — for example, someone bleeding from the neck."}, {"time": 455.26, "label": "7:35", "text": "Yellow Zone: for serious injuries that can wait briefly, like abdominal wounds without active bleeding."}, {"time": 463.34, "label": "7:43", "text": "Green Zone: for minor injuries such as glass cuts or superficial shrapnel wounds."}, {"time": 471.02, "label": "7:51", "text": "Black Zone: for those who are already deceased or beyond saving."}, {"time": 477.02, "label": "7:57", "text": "Each zone is staffed by a full medical team: an attending physician, a nurse, a resident, and medical students. Triage officers are responsible for deciding which patient goes where, based on the severity of their condition. And when surgery is required, the trauma team calls in the appropriate specialist: orthopedic surgeons for limb injuries, cardiothoracic surgeons for chest trauma, neurosurgeons for brain injuries, and so on."}, {"time": 513.62, "label": "8:33", "text": "They don’t call every doctor. They directly contact the heads of 7–8 critical divisions, who then mobilize their teams."}, {"time": 522.76, "label": "8:42", "text": "This structure ensures that teams work in parallel, not chaos. Without it, you cannot manage hundreds of patients — like the 370 people injured in the Beirut blast. Each team focuses on its zone, and senior leaders, like the heads of the emergency and surgery departments, circulate constantly to monitor everything. Behind all of this is a clearly defined Emergency Preparedness Plan (EPP)"}, {"time": 555.62, "label": "9:15", "text": "But no plan is ever perfect. I wanted to know — does this strategy change from one crisis to another? After a mass casualty event like the Pager explosion, did the team  find weaknesses in the system? And if so, how do they fix them?"}, {"time": 573.8, "label": "9:33", "text": "During the Pager explosion, one major challenge we faced was imaging. At the time, our ER had only one CT scanner, and we received over 180 patients with shrapnel injuries to the eyes and limbs — all requiring immediate scans. The other two CT scanners in the hospital, located in the main imaging department downstairs, were still operating on a normal schedule for outpatient appointments, so we couldn’t rely on them."}, {"time": 606.38, "label": "10:06", "text": "After that experience, we revised our emergency protocol. Now, in the event of another mass casualty incident, we plan to immediately cancel all outpatient appointments and clear all three CT scanners — the one in the ER and the two downstairs — so they’re fully dedicated to trauma patients. With this setup, we can triple our imaging capacity in a crisis and avoid the bottlenecks we experienced that day."}, {"time": 637.08, "label": "10:37", "text": "A decade ago, we didn’t have ultrasound in the ER, so identifying internal bleeding was difficult. Today, we use the FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) scan — a rapid bedside ultrasound that allows us to quickly detect organ damage and bleeding, helping us make faster, life-saving decisions.Blood readiness was another challenge. In the past, we’d have to wait to test blood types before transfusions — but bleeding patients can’t wait. So we launched a Massive Transfusion Protocol that ensures blood and platelets, including O-negative, are prepared in advance. It’s one of the most important upgrades we’ve made.Each time we find a weakness, we build a solution. For example, we created a task force called FEMEA to study delays between ER admission and surgery. Their goal is to reduce that time from 45 minutes to 15 minutes. It’s a huge effort — but it can mean the difference between saving a life or not."}, {"time": 713.56, "label": "11:53", "text": "We also run regular simulation drills, where we act out a full-scale mass casualty scenario to test our systems. We identify delays — maybe the elevator gets stuck, maybe imaging backs up — and we fix them before the next real emergency."}, {"time": 732.98, "label": "12:12", "text": "We don’t just hope to be ready. We train for it, over and over again.”"}, {"time": 738.94, "label": "12:18", "text": "Hearing how each experience led to concrete improvements thst made me wonder about the bigger picture.Beyond individual hospitals, can Lebanon — after everything it has endured — build a healthcare system that truly supports trauma survivors?”"}, {"time": 756.62, "label": "12:36", "text": "“I believe Lebanon can build an effective healthcare system — but it requires organization and national coordination. It’s not just about how many hospitals or doctors we have; what important  is how we structure and manage the system."}, {"time": 775.88, "label": "12:55", "text": "The Ministry of Health cannot do everything alone. Universities, research centers, and medical institutions need to collaborate. We must conduct field assessments to study hospital readiness — looking at operating rooms, emergency protocols, and equipment — and identify gaps."}, {"time": 798.06, "label": "13:18", "text": "There’s no such thing as 'it can’t be fixed.' Every crisis we’ve faced — from the 2006 war to the Beirut blast — has taught us lessons. During the  2006 war, we performed surgeries underground for 33 days. During the Beirut explosion, hospitals and medical staff were themselves victims — yet we continued to operate."}, {"time": 828.96, "label": "13:48", "text": "Each mass casualty event must be treated as an opportunity to improve, not just to recover."}, {"time": 836.32, "label": "13:56", "text": "Trauma doesn’t only affect the wounded — it affects families, communities, and society as a whole. One injury can ripple through an entire family’s future."}, {"time": 850.38, "label": "14:10", "text": "So yes, Lebanon can have a strong trauma care system. But it requires smarter planning, stronger emergency preparedness plans like the EPP, faster protocols, and continuous training."}, {"time": 868.06, "label": "14:28", "text": "We don’t wait for the next disaster to learn.We simulate.We adapt.Because every second matters — and every life is worth saving.”"}, {"time": 881.14, "label": "14:41", "text": "What stood out to me in Dr. Abi Saad’s words was not just resilience — but a deep belief in learning from every crisis.Building a stronger healthcare system isn’t just about surviving disasters. It’s about being ready, staying ready, and protecting every life — no matter the circumstances."}, {"time": 903.38, "label": "15:03", "text": "But survival is only the beginning. For many patients, the real journey starts after the emergency — when they must learn to live with an amputation and navigate the long road of physical and emotional recovery."}, {"time": 919.36, "label": "15:19", "text": "To better understand what happens after the trauma, I spoke with Specialist Bassam Singer, the owner of Center Singer for Prosthetics and Orthotics.We talked about the challenges of accessing prosthetic care in Lebanon, the technologies that are available, and the deep personal impact of losing — and regaining — mobility."}, {"time": 941.1, "label": "15:41", "text": "Dr. Singer began by explaining the foundation of his work — and the difference between prosthetics and orthotics."}, {"time": 949.68, "label": "15:49", "text": "At our center, we specialize in two main areas: prosthetics, which deal with amputations, and orthotics, which support patients with conditions like scoliosis, paralysis, or neuromuscular disorders. In those cases, we create customized orthopedic devices to help with posture, mobility, and muscle compensation."}, {"time": 986.1, "label": "16:26", "text": "But prosthetics and orthotics alone are not enough. Our work always includes physiotherapy because every device must be paired with physical preparation and retraining. Before fitting a prosthetic, we begin strengthening the body, improving balance, and teaching the patient how to adapt. This applies to every patient, but especially to children with conditions like cerebral palsy."}, {"time": 1000.82, "label": "16:40", "text": "From a technology standpoint, we’re fully up to date. We use CAD-CAM systems and 3D scanning to create customized devices based on the patient's exact measurements. For upper limbs, we use myoelectric prosthetics. We place electrodes on the muscles, and when the brain sends a signal, like “open hand” — the prosthetic responds. in some casesb If the nerves in the arm stump are too weak, we reroute signals from a stronger area — like the shoulder — to restore control."}, {"time": 1037.46, "label": "17:17", "text": "For lower limbs, we use microprocessor-controlled prosthetics. These are programmed through dedicated software and can even be adjusted using a mobile app."}, {"time": 1049.4, "label": "17:29", "text": "These technologies give us powerful tools to rebuild motion."}, {"time": 1053.7, "label": "17:33", "text": "We also offer aesthetic prosthetics in special cases. After the Pager explosion, for example, we treated a patient who had lost several fingers. In those situations, we create silicone replicas that match the original hand in shape, color, texture, and even hair. These prosthetics aren’t functional — they don’t move — but they restore confidence and visual normalcy."}, {"time": 1083.16, "label": "18:03", "text": "Dr. Singer’s team is clearly at the cutting edge — but not every patient has access to that level of care. So I asked him about the cost of prosthetics in Lebanon, and who can actually afford them."}, {"time": 1098.86, "label": "18:18", "text": "The price of a prosthetic depends on many factors — the patient’s age, physical condition, lifestyle, and whether they need something basic or advanced. Devices can range from $1,500 Up to one hundred ten thousand U.S. dollars $110,000."}, {"time": 1117.18, "label": "18:37", "text": "If a 70-year-old patient needs a leg just to get to the bathroom, we recommend something simple. But if a young, active person comes in — and they can afford it — we fit them with a smart prosthetic. If not, we offer a basic, mechanical one that still meets essential needs."}, {"time": 1138.14, "label": "18:58", "text": "For children, it’s more complicated. They outgrow their prosthetics quickly. The socket needs to be replaced every three months. The entire limb — every six months. That makes pediatric care much more expensive, even if we don’t use advanced technology."}, {"time": 1158.4, "label": "19:18", "text": "And unfortunately, there’s no governmental support. The Lebanese Army is the only exception, and even their budget is extremely limited now. Social Security and the Ministry of Health don’t classify prosthetics as essential. They treat it as a secondary or non-essential service—what they often call an “extra” or add-on—so they don’t allocate budget for it."}, {"time": 1185.36, "label": "19:45", "text": "Despite all the technology and skill, Dr. Singer emphasized that the most powerful part of his work isn’t mechanical — it’s emotional."}, {"time": 1195.68, "label": "19:55", "text": "A prosthetic isn’t just a medical device. It’s a process — and it’s deeply emotional. Many patients arrive at our center in wheelchairs, emotionally devastated. But when they finally stand up, walk, or regain a small motion — the moment is overwhelming. I’ve seen people cry, laugh, freeze in disbelief. It’s the moment they realize their life is not over."}, {"time": 1225.26, "label": "20:25", "text": "But getting to that point isn’t easy. Rehabilitation is the real challenge. The first stage is pre-fitting physiotherapy. We help the patient rebuild strength, balance, and coordination — preparing them to support themselves. Only after this phase do we start training with the prosthetic."}, {"time": 1247.38, "label": "20:47", "text": "The entire adaptation process typically takes one week to a month, depending on the person. Most patients visit us three to four times per week, with each session lasting up to four hours. Every case is different, and progress depends on physical strength, psychological readiness, and personal motivation."}, {"time": 1270.0, "label": "21:10", "text": "For children, the process is even more demanding. Their bodies are still growing, which means more frequent changes — and more physical and emotional support. But the impact of helping a child walk again? It’s something you never forget."}, {"time": 1289.0, "label": "21:29", "text": "As our conversation came to a close, I asked Dr. Singer to look ahead — not just at the next patient walking through his door, but at the bigger picture. What does the future hold for prosthetic care in Lebanon, a country full of talent but short on support?"}, {"time": 1309.12, "label": "21:49", "text": "The technology is here. We stay up to date with every advancement. At our center, we follow the global innovation in prosthetics — we use scanning, software, smart systems. We’re ready to lead."}, {"time": 1325.6, "label": "22:05", "text": "And we have the talent. Lebanon has students, engineers, and specialists with incredible potential. I’ve worked with young professionals who could easily compete internationally — not just in fitting prosthetics, but in designing and building them. From a human resources perspective, we’re not behind."}, {"time": 1349.34, "label": "22:29", "text": "But even with all our technology and talent, we’re still working within the limitations of a country facing deep economic and structural challenges. Lebanon is a resilient nation — but it’s also a fragile one. And that’s why, today, there’s still no national policy or sustainable funding to support prosthetic care."}, {"time": 1372.62, "label": "22:52", "text": "In many countries, the healthcare system ensures that prosthetics are fully covered. Here, patients often have to navigate the process on their own. The public sector does not yet treat prosthetics as a guaranteed right — and that leaves many people behind."}, {"time": 1391.54, "label": "23:11", "text": "At the same time, we’re doing what we can with what we have. While we currently import all the electronic components, joints, and materials, we do manufacture the socket locally — the part that connects the prosthetic to the patient’s body. It’s a start. And it’s a sign that with the right investment and coordination, Lebanon could one day expand its local production — reducing costs, increasing access, and becoming a regional leader in prosthetic innovation."}, {"time": 1429.56, "label": "23:49", "text": "Listening to Dr. Singer, one thing becomes clear: even in a fragile system, progress is possible. Piece by piece, socket by socket, a future is being built — not just in technology, but in determination."}, {"time": 1446.9, "label": "24:06", "text": "Prosthetics in Lebanon may still depend on imported parts, but the spirit behind them is entirely local — driven by doctors, engineers, physiotherapists, and patients who refuse to give up."}, {"time": 1462.06, "label": "24:22", "text": "As I wrap up this podcast, I find myself thinking less about endings, and more about continuations."}, {"time": 1470.42, "label": "24:30", "text": "Because stories like these don’t stop when the patient is discharged. They don’t stop at the prosthetic fitting, or the physical therapy session. They continue — in every step taken, every skill relearned, and every system quietly being improved by those who believe that mobility is a right, not a privilege."}, {"time": 1493.44, "label": "24:53", "text": "What I’ve learned in this journey so far is that Lebanon is not lacking in brilliance. It’s not lacking in drive, in expertise, or in compassion. What it needs is coordination. Vision. And the courage to treat recovery as a collective responsibility."}, {"time": 1514.72, "label": "25:14", "text": "And I’m not done asking questions."}, {"time": 1517.54, "label": "25:17", "text": "In the coming weeks, I’ll be continuing this project — not through a podcast series, but through deeper conversations and expanded research. I’ve already scheduled an interview with Dr. Jamal Hoballah, Chairperson and Professor of Surgery at AUBMC. With his expertise in vascular surgery, stents, and reperfusion injury, I want to better understand the decision-making behind amputations — and how the body, and the system, can prepare for healing before the wound even closes."}, {"time": 1554.3, "label": "25:54", "text": "I’m also planning to reach out to the Center for the Protection of the Rights of the Disabled — to explore the legal, social, and emotional landscapes that shape the lives of amputees after they leave the hospital. What protections exist under Lebanese law? What barriers — financial, infrastructural, or cultural — still stand in the way of full reintegration? I want to understand how issues like health insurance coverage, employment rights, access to public space, and the cost of long-term prosthetic maintenance affect people’s ability not just to survive, but to truly participate in society. Because recovery doesn’t end with physical healing. It continues in the systems that either support or neglect those trying to rebuild their lives."}, {"time": 1610.0, "label": "26:50", "text": "This project began with a question about prosthetics. But what it’s really about — is people. Systems. And the silent strength it takes to rebuild not just a limb, but a life."}, {"time": 1624.08, "label": "27:04", "text": "Thank you for listening. And thank you for walking this path with me."}];

// Curatorial podcast descriptions supplied 11 October 2026.
Object.entries({
"the-body-remembers": "Seungju Moon's podcast explores how war in Lebanon leaves lasting traces in the body and mind. Through conversations with Dalaa Zaarour and clinical psychologist Charlotte El-Khalil, Moon brings personal testimony into dialogue with research to examine embodied trauma, the limits of diagnostic labels, and the barriers to seeking care.",
"rebuilt-lives": "Hawraa Ayoub's podcast examines the challenges of medical care and long-term rehabilitation following Lebanon’s repeated mass-casualty events, from the 2006 war and the Beirut port explosion in 2020 to the 2024 pager attacks. Drawing on interviews with George Abi Saad, a trauma surgeon at AUBMC, and prosthetics specialist Bassam Singer, the podcast explores the gap between emergency medical intervention and sustained recovery, particularly for amputees. It highlights the financial, institutional, and social barriers to prosthetic care, raising a fundamental question: Is rehabilitation in Lebanon a right, or a privilege determined by one’s ability to pay?",
"negotiated-care": "Marilynn Abou Zeidan's podcast examines how access to healthcare in Lebanon is shaped by institutional fragmentation, financial pressures, and informal networks known as \"wasta.\" Through interviews with a former Minister of Public Health, a patient, and an insurance-sector representative, Abou Zeidan explores the distance between formal coverage and the lived experience of obtaining treatment.",
"war-medicine": "Nizar Jaber's podcast examines what happens to medicine when the systems that normally support care are disrupted by war. Drawing on interviews with surgeons, a medical student, and a civilian affected by the September 2024 pager explosions, the podcast explores how scarcity, mass casualties, danger, and damaged infrastructure reshape clinical practice. It considers the ethical challenges of triage and resource allocation, the moral burden placed on healthcare workers, the disruption of medical education, and the difficulty of preserving patients’ narratives during emergencies."
}).forEach(([id, synopsis]) => { window.EXHIBITION.works.find(work => work.id === id).synopsis = synopsis; });

// Approved documentary description, 11 October 2026.
window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w => w.id === 'burnout-in-medicine').synopsis = 'Lea Halabi’s documentary examines physician burnout in Lebanon amid war, economic collapse, the COVID-19 pandemic, and the Beirut port explosion. Through interviews with emergency physicians Tharwat El Zahran and Rachelle El Helou, and AUB President Fadlo R. Khuri, Halabi explores the personal and professional pressures of providing care through overlapping crises, alongside the experiences and sources of support that help sustain those responsible for saving lives.';

Object.assign(window.EXHIBITION.works.find(w=>w.id==="burnout-in-medicine"),{"shareUrl": "https://mailaub-my.sharepoint.com/:v:/g/personal/raa189_mail_aub_edu/IQBUZqOsYcumRp8H9dsV3yNmAQbsBUgynAL2p7szzyNtcFg?e=MrmdWM", "poster": "lea-burnout-cover.jpg", "posterAlt": "A healthcare worker in blue scrubs sits against a hospital corridor wall with a hand over their face; other staff stand in the background.", "credits": "Annotated Bibliography\nTitle: Burnout in Medicine: The Cost of Saving Lives in Lebanon\n\nInterviews (Primary Sources): \nDr. Tharwat El Zahran\nAssociate Professor of Clinical Emergency Medicine \nDirector of Clinical Operations for the Department of Emergency Medicine \nCo-director of Toxicology Service, Emergency Department ​\nShe shared her experience as an emergency physician\nDr. Rachelle El Helou\nInstructor of Clinical Emergency Medicine \nProgram Director of Pediatric Emergency Medicine Fellowship \nAssociate Program Director of Emergency Medicine \nQuality Director of Pediatric Emergency Medicine\nShe discussed factors in her life that helped prevent burnout\n\nFadlo R. Khuri\nPresident of the American University of Beirut\nHe shared the overall experience and timeline of the chaos\nSecondary Sources: \n\nHitti, E., Cheaito, M. A., & Kazzi, A. A. (2023). Beirut port blast 2020: New lessons learned in mass casualty incident management in the emergency department. Journal of Emergency Medicine, 65(6), e580–e583. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jemermed.2023.07.012\nThis article was very meaningful to read and helped me come up with the questions used in the interview. Dr. Hitti reflected on her personal experience during the beirut blast.\nPress, E. (2023, June 15). The moral crisis of America's doctors. The New York Times Magazine. https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/15/magazine/doctors-moral-crises.html\n This article was assigned to be read in class. It explored themes of moral injury and physician burnout in the US which pushed me forward to look into the topic in the Lebanese context.\nAl Hariri, M., Hamade, B., Bizri, M., Salman, O. F., Tamim, H. M., & Al Jalbout, N. (2022, January 1). Psychological impact of covid-19 on emergency department healthcare workers in a tertiary care center during a national economic crisis. AUB ScholarWorks Home. https://scholarworks.aub.edu.lb/items/80b31194-aa92-4fa6-b5f7-b85dd81ae812 \nThis study explored the different events that were occurring at once like the financial strain and Covid-19 pandemic and how it related to levels of anxiety and burnout among staff. \nFiller Clips as B-Rolls:\nhttps://youtu.be/pf4J8xgq2-8?si=hB73nyP2L1sn12Tz\nhttps://youtu.be/irOUCHyKTts?si=5nrt5Z4RbX6F19Oh\nhttps://youtu.be/OhcH-aF3zSU?si=be_rL_1q0X2g3Xx7\nhttps://youtu.be/ZFXCTQDNfUU?si=l6JCBbiREo1jo34N\nhttps://youtu.be/zIaSTozGOmI?si=Fgg002IKZuHzzb_R\nhttps://youtu.be/qtyTOD2IC2A?si=9QwGv31WaDOjmOVZ\nhttps://youtu.be/w4MOObwBeSg?si=sPR3N1ztByxoqF9a\nhttps://youtu.be/cyFmpz2CS0I?si=oPEXcO0yrUnD4wc9\nhttps://youtu.be/BQp79UuctlM?si=MUhL4ZLaiHxYhJtE\nhttps://youtu.be/HEnsUftrYG4?si=8HWQfpKmdWFJ_LxB\nhttps://youtu.be/ecZp15k4HnQ?si=RMpziXLOU-7LKCwa\nhttps://youtu.be/yNlis-R4gKo?si=Ev8pZU-_UJHdENnI\nhttps://youtu.be/NA5cC8rTbag?si=Pzr0m1PUCdGoLV27\nhttps://youtu.be/XhUh2Gfq2hM?si=hrPUAuLfLQtVIt_n\nhttps://youtu.be/S2jnnwoTI-I?si=-utR2BSxyWKcepZy\nhttps://youtu.be/CYmuK_pLIbQ?si=bk-gQlaA6ucTVv6H\nhttps://youtu.be/EkJBN9XPp-o?si=1tO1wqQIRE6bL9yx\nhttps://youtu.be/bfOiU5zAKAo?si=itPybtcH_SLKccxb\nhttps://youtu.be/4hfzpGHFlvw?si=WXULF7KNJjmIemJD\nhttps://youtu.be/Ew4T3P-bv_Y?si=NPPYvWHsbMTCZjcO\nhttps://youtu.be/xKPWngYf2Wk?si=ccyS18n-18i4-HwY\nhttps://youtu.be/KQK0TUkCi9A?si=xu95Mz6O3H2m1bgC\nhttps://youtu.be/s_dJWqScFMM?si=gyzWa68BFE-LwNks\n", "approved": true, "embedUrl": "", "src": "", "coverCredit": "[Cover credit to be supplied]", "duration": ""});
